{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.24056","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.24056","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.24056","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tung-Ling","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tung-Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongliang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hongliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Logit-Gap Steering: A Forward-Pass Diagnostic for Alignment Robustness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T17:01:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:30:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-01T19:27:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-05T00:08:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-05T05:06:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-06T01:56:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-09T22:27:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-14T00:18:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"RLHF-style alignment trains language models to refuse unsafe requests, but how much operational margin does this refusal rest on? We introduce the refusal-affirmation logit gap: the difference between the top refusal-token logit and the top affirmative-token logit at the first decoding step. This single scalar quantifies the per-prompt safety margin that alignment provides. Empirically, alignment widens the gap on 97.5-99.8% of toxic prompts across three model families, and median gap closure co-varies with True-ASR ranking across suffix strategies (an internal consistency check, since our method optimises gap closure). To validate the metric's practical significance, we present logit-gap steering, a gradient-free, forward-pass-only method that discovers short in-distribution suffixes ($\u0026lt;$10 tokens per component) whose cumulative effect closes the gap. The method requires ${\\approx}26{,}000$ forward-pass equivalents per family (${\\approx}2$~min on one A100), ${\\approx}125\\times$ less than a single GCG search. Suffixes discovered on 0.5B--2B models transfer without modification to 72B within family. An 8-suffix ensemble reaches 38-96\\% True ASR across 13 models on AdvBench and HarmBench, with most suffixes having $10^{3}$-$10^{4}\\times$ lower perplexity than GCG-meaning published perplexity-filter defenses that collapse GCG (64.7%$\\to$1.0%) leave our suffixes nearly intact (76.9%$\\to$76.0%). These results demonstrate that current alignment margins, while consistently present, can be thin and efficiently measurable, and that defense strategies must account for in-distribution suffixes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2026 Main Track (poster). Camera-ready version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.24056","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T03:00:53Z","registered":"2025-07-01T03:00:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:17:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22957","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22957","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22957","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Younwoo","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Younwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changling","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Changling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongjin","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yongjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijing","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Zhijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agent-to-Agent Theory of Mind: Testing Interlocutor Awareness among Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-28T17:22:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:29:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-27T20:38:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-05T00:56:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.1471","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) are increasingly integrated into multi-agent and human-AI systems, understanding their awareness of both self-context and conversational partners is essential for ensuring reliable performance and robust safety. While prior work has extensively studied situational awareness which refers to an LLM's ability to recognize its operating phase and constraints, it has largely overlooked the complementary capacity to identify and adapt to the identity and characteristics of a dialogue partner. In this paper, we formalize this latter capability as interlocutor awareness and present the first systematic evaluation of its emergence in contemporary LLMs. We examine interlocutor inference across three dimensions-reasoning patterns, linguistic style, and alignment preferences-and show that LLMs reliably identify same-family peers and certain prominent model families, such as GPT and Claude. To demonstrate its practical significance, we develop three case studies in which interlocutor awareness both enhances multi-LLM collaboration through prompt adaptation and introduces new alignment and safety vulnerabilities, including reward-hacking behaviors and increased jailbreak susceptibility. Our findings highlight the dual promise and peril of identity-sensitive behavior in LLMs, underscoring the need for further understanding of interlocutor awareness and new safeguards in multi-agent deployments. Our code is open-sourced at https://github.com/younwoochoi/InterlocutorAwarenessLLM."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22957","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-07-01T02:34:35Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:34:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-05T02:42:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23749","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23749","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23749","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Boyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijian","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Zijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengling","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Fengling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bach","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Bach","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lingming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tegawendé F.","familyName":"Bissyandé","name":"Bissyandé, Tegawendé F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoye","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Haoye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Survey of LLM-based Automated Program Repair: Taxonomies, Design Paradigms, and Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T11:46:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:14:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-04T08:21:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-05T01:28:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T07:31:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-28T00:33:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are reshaping automated program repair. We present a reproducible hierarchical taxonomy that organizes 66 LLM-based repair systems according to where repair capability and control logic principally reside: task-adapted parameters, prompt and context design, designer-specified workflows, or LLM-directed runtime control. Adaptation, generation pattern, runtime control, and auxiliary evidence are preserved as separate coded dimensions. This representation exposes control distinctions hidden by utilization labels and supports cross-paradigm analysis of system design and evaluation evidence. To the best of our knowledge, it is the first publicly available LLM-based software repair survey to operationalize Fine-Tuning, Prompting, Procedural, and Agentic as one corpus-wide primary classification. Our hierarchy complements prior surveys through one corpus-wide primary decision rule, while the result-level protocol audit bounds which reported results are defensibly comparable. To analyze how repair systems use benchmarks, we record benchmark variants, metrics, base models, and fault-localization assumptions for each system's primary reported result, identifying protocol-aligned comparison windows and benchmark-family fragments. We clarify paradigm trade-offs in task alignment, deployment cost, controllability, and multi-hunk or cross-file repair. We outline open challenges and research directions. Our artifacts and scripted survey pipeline are publicly available at https://github.com/GLEAM-Lab/ProgramRepair."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23749","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:53:32Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:53:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:39:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23921","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23921","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23921","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Germans","familyName":"Savcisens","name":"Savcisens, Germans","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tina","familyName":"Eliassi-Rad","name":"Eliassi-Rad, Tina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Trilemma of Truth in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.7; G.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T50","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T14:49:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:23:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-08T21:09:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:06:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-10T21:06:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-12T01:08:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-14T22:47:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-11-18T01:13:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-22T16:41:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06-23T02:53:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The public often attributes human-like qualities to large language models (LLMs), assuming that they \"know\" certain things. In reality, LLMs encode information retained during training as internal probabilistic knowledge. This study examines existing methods for probing the veracity of that knowledge and identifies three flawed underlying assumptions. To address these flaws, we introduce sAwMIL (Sparse-Aware Multiple-Instance Learning), a multiclass probing framework that combines multiple-instance learning with conformal prediction. sAwMIL leverages LLMs' internal representations to classify statements as true, false, or neither. We evaluate sAwMIL across 16 open-source LLMs, including default and chat-based variants, using three new curated datasets. Our results show that (1) common probing methods fail to provide a reliable and transferable veracity direction and, in some settings, perform worse than zero-shot prompting; (2) truth and falsehood are not encoded symmetrically; and (3) LLMs encode a third type of signal that is distinct from both true and false."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The main text is 9 pages long (plus 3 pages of references); supplementary material (60 pages) is included in the same PDF"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23921","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":25,"citationCount":2,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-07-01T02:57:36Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:57:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-23T05:32:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23274","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23274","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23274","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hans Peter Lyngsøe","familyName":"Raaschou-Jensen","name":"Raaschou-Jensen, Hans Peter Lyngsøe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Constanza","familyName":"Fierro","name":"Fierro, Constanza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anders","familyName":"Søgaard","name":"Søgaard, Anders","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Real-Time Progress Prediction in Reasoning Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-29T15:01:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:46:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-01T09:46:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:43:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T12:11:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:43:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-26T11:19:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-27T00:51:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent reasoning language models, particularly those that employ long latent chains of thought, achieve strong performance on complex agentic tasks. However, as these models operate over increasingly long time horizons, their internal progress becomes opaque to users, making expectation management and real-time oversight difficult. In this work, we investigate whether real-time progress prediction is feasible for such models. We first test whether hidden states encode progress information by discretizing reasoning trajectories and training a linear probe to classify reasoning states. We then fine-tune models to generate progress estimates from 0--100\\% during chain-of-thought reasoning. Our strongest progress-reporting checkpoint reaches 0.161 MAE on mathematical reasoning traces and outperforms position baselines in this setting. Finally, we quantify the intrinsic ambiguity of progress labels by measuring how much the implied progress value varies from the same partial rollout. This ambiguity is lowest for Qwen3-4B, whose continuations produce the smallest rollout dispersion, suggesting that larger models can make progress labels more stable by reducing variation in remaining solution length."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23274","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:42:08Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:42:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-27T02:34:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22598","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22598","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22598","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Edwards","name":"Edwards, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukyung","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Yukyung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujun Audrey","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Yujun Audrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulu","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Yulu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Schuster","name":"Schuster, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Najoung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Najoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RExBench: Can coding agents autonomously implement AI research extensions?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T19:41:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:05:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-17T18:45:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-21T00:04:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T20:17:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-23T00:08:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agents based on Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown promise for performing sophisticated software engineering tasks autonomously. In addition, there has been progress towards developing agents that can perform parts of the research pipeline in machine learning and the natural sciences. We argue that research extension and its implementation is a critical capability for such systems, and introduce RExBench to support the evaluation of this capability. RExBench is a benchmark consisting of realistic extensions of 12 research papers that aim to investigate novel research hypotheses. Each task is set up as an extension to an existing research paper and codebase, accompanied by domain expert-written instructions. RExBench is robust to data contamination and supports an automatic evaluation infrastructure that executes agent outputs to determine whether the success criteria are met. We use this benchmark to evaluate 12 LLM agents implemented using two different frameworks, aider and OpenHands. We find that all agents fail to autonomously implement the majority of the extensions, with the best agent achieving around a 33% success rate. Although the success rate improves with additional human-written hints, the best performance under this setting remains below 44%. This indicates that current agents are still short of being able to handle realistic research extension tasks without substantial human guidance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22598","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:26:03Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:26:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-23T02:20:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.24106","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.24106","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.24106","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanhong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yanhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karen","familyName":"Livescu","name":"Livescu, Karen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Predictive Power of Representation Dispersion in Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T17:53:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:33:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-20T14:35:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T02:17:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We show that a language model's ability to predict text is tightly linked to the breadth of its embedding space: models that spread their contextual representations more widely tend to achieve lower perplexity. Concretely, we find that representation dispersion--the average pairwise cosine distance among hidden vectors--strongly and negatively correlates with perplexity across diverse model families (LLaMA, Qwen, and others) and domains (Wikipedia, news, scientific abstracts). Beyond illustrating this link, we show how dispersion can be leveraged for a range of practical tasks--without requiring labeled data. First, measuring dispersion on unlabeled text allows us to rank examples by difficulty and identify hard slices in new domains, offering a data-efficient tool for screening and prioritizing models before full evaluation. Next, we find that identifying layers with higher dispersion pinpoints the best representations for retrieval-based methods such as kNN-LM, bypassing exhaustive layer-by-layer searches. Finally, we integrate a simple \"push-away\" objective into training, which increases dispersion in both single-domain and cross-domain scenarios and directly improves perplexity in each. Code is available at https://github.com/yanhong-lbh/rep_dispersion."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.24106","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T03:02:05Z","registered":"2025-07-01T03:02:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:46:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23919","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23919","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23919","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haonan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Haonan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingxiang","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Jingxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bangjun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bangjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianrui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuchuan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xuchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boren","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Boren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwen","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Yiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenrui","familyName":"Tie","name":"Tie, Chenrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Goal-VLA: Image-Generative VLMs as Object-Centric World Models Empowering Zero-shot Robot Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T14:48:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:23:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-30T15:25:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-22T00:08:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-30T01:48:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-31T01:14:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generalization remains a fundamental challenge in robotic manipulation. To tackle this challenge, recent Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models build policies on top of Vision-Language Models (VLMs), seeking to transfer their open-world semantic knowledge. However, their zero-shot capability lags significantly behind the base VLMs, as the instruction-vision-action data is too limited to cover diverse scenarios, tasks, and robot embodiments. In this work, we present Goal-VLA, a zero-shot framework that leverages Image-Generative VLMs as world models to generate desired goal states, from which the target object pose is derived to enable generalizable manipulation. The key insight is that object state representation is the golden interface, naturally separating a manipulation system into high-level and low-level policies. This representation abstracts away explicit action annotations, allowing the use of highly generalizable VLMs while simultaneously providing spatial cues for training-free low-level control. To further improve robustness, we introduce a Reflection-through-Synthesis process that iteratively validates and refines the generated goal image before execution. Both simulated and real-world experiments demonstrate that our \\name achieves strong performance and inspiring generalizability in manipulation tasks. Supplementary materials are available at https://nus-lins-lab.github.io/goalvlaweb/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23919","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:57:33Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:57:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-31T03:59:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.24119","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.24119","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.24119","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leon","familyName":"Guertler","name":"Guertler, Leon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zichen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Penghui","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Penghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Balcells","name":"Balcells, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mickel","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mickel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheston","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Cheston","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiyan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Weiyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wee Sun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Wee Sun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Natasha","familyName":"Jaques","name":"Jaques, Natasha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SPIRAL: Self-Play on Zero-Sum Games Incentivizes Reasoning via Multi-Agent Multi-Turn Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T17:58:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:34:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T02:29:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-02T00:19:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T14:09:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T03:10:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in reinforcement learning have shown that language models can develop sophisticated reasoning through training on tasks with verifiable rewards, but these approaches depend on human-curated problem-answer pairs and domain-specific reward engineering. We introduce SPIRAL, a self-play framework where models learn by playing multi-turn, zero-sum games against continuously improving versions of themselves, generating an automatic curriculum of stronger opponents, and eliminating the need for human supervision. To enable this self-play training at scale, we implement a fully online, multi-turn, multi-agent reinforcement learning system for LLMs and propose role-conditioned advantage estimation (RAE) to stabilize multi-agent training. SPIRAL produces reasoning capabilities that transfer broadly, improving performance by up to 10% across a suite of 8 reasoning benchmarks on 4 different models spanning Qwen and Llama model families, outperforming supervised fine-tuning on 25,000 expert game trajectories. Multi-game training (TicTacToe, Kuhn Poker, Simple Negotiation) yields the strongest results, with improvements observed across both base and instruction-tuned models. Analysis of chain-of-thought traces reveals that games develop distinct cognitive patterns that transfer to improve reasoning performance, with different games developing complementary strengths. Even models which have already been trained on reasoning tasks using RLVR, like DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, still benefit from our approach. These results demonstrate that zero-sum games naturally develop transferable reasoning capabilities across diverse model architectures and training stages, highlighting a promising direction for autonomous reasoning development. Our code can be found in https://github.com/spiral-rl/spiral."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026. Code: https://github.com/spiral-rl/spiral"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.24119","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T03:02:24Z","registered":"2025-07-01T03:02:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:39:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23979","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23979","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23979","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renren","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Renren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianhao","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Tianhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinwei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xinwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqi","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Yuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wuwei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Wuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quandong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Quandong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Luan","name":"Luan, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deyi","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Deyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TaP: A Taxonomy-Guided Framework for Automated and Scalable Preference Data Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T15:45:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:26:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-16T07:56:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-17T01:29:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-17T10:47:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-18T01:36:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-21T17:30:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:32:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Conducting supervised and preference fine-tuning of large language models (LLMs) requires high-quality datasets to improve their ability to follow instructions and align with human preferences and values. However, constructing such datasets is resource-intensive, and most publicly available datasets are in English. To address these challenges, we propose the \\underline{\\textbf{Ta}}xonomy-Guided \\underline{\\textbf{P}}reference Data Generation (TaP) framework for automated, scalable preference dataset construction across languages. TaP uses a structured taxonomy to provide fine-grained control over dataset composition, ensuring diversity and broad coverage. We use TaP-generated datasets to perform supervised and preference fine-tuning on multiple LLMs. Experimental results demonstrate that LLMs trained on TaP-generated datasets outperform those trained on existing open-source datasets. Remarkably, LLMs trained on TaP-generated datasets outperform models trained on an open-source dataset that is 180$\\times$ larger."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages, 16 tables, 10 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23979","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:58:58Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:58:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T03:56:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23951","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23951","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23951","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathis Le","familyName":"Bail","name":"Bail, Mathis Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jérémie","familyName":"Dentan","name":"Dentan, Jérémie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davide","familyName":"Buscaldi","name":"Buscaldi, Davide","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sonia","familyName":"Vanier","name":"Vanier, Sonia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unveiling Decision-Making in LLMs for Text Classification : Extraction of influential and interpretable concepts with Sparse Autoencoders"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T15:18:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:25:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-20T13:07:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-23T01:33:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs) have been successfully used to probe Large Language Models (LLMs) and extract interpretable concepts from their internal representations. These concepts are linear combinations of neuron activations that correspond to human-interpretable features. In this paper, we investigate the effectiveness of SAE-based explainability approaches for sentence classification, a domain where such methods have not been extensively explored. We present a novel SAE-based model ClassifSAE tailored for text classification, leveraging a specialized classifier head and incorporating an activation rate sparsity loss. We benchmark this architecture against established methods such as ConceptShap, Independent Component Analysis, HI-Concept and a standard TopK-SAE baseline. Our evaluation covers several classification benchmarks and backbone LLMs. We further enrich our analysis with two novel metrics for measuring the precision of concept-based explanations, using an external sentence encoder. Our empirical results show that ClassifSAE improves both the causality and interpretability of the extracted features."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23951","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:58:18Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:58:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-23T02:43:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23260","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23260","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23260","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed Amine","familyName":"Ferrag","name":"Ferrag, Mohamed Amine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Norbert","familyName":"Tihanyi","name":"Tihanyi, Norbert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Djallel","familyName":"Hamouda","name":"Hamouda, Djallel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leandros","familyName":"Maglaras","name":"Maglaras, Leandros","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abderrahmane","familyName":"Lakas","name":"Lakas, Abderrahmane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Merouane","familyName":"Debbah","name":"Debbah, Merouane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Prompt Injections to Protocol Exploits: Threats in LLM-Powered AI Agents Workflows"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-29T14:32:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:46:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-14T20:29:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-16T02:03:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.icte.2025.12.001","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autonomous AI agents powered by large language models (LLMs) with structured function-calling interfaces enable real-time data retrieval, computation, and multi-step orchestration. However, the rapid growth of plugins, connectors, and inter-agent protocols has outpaced security practices, leading to brittle integrations that rely on ad-hoc authentication, inconsistent schemas, and weak validation. This survey introduces a unified end-to-end threat model for LLM-agent ecosystems, covering host-to-tool and agent-to-agent communications. We systematically categorize more than thirty attack techniques spanning input manipulation, model compromise, system and privacy attacks, and protocol-level vulnerabilities. For each category, we provide a formal threat formulation defining attacker capabilities, objectives, and affected system layers. Representative examples include Prompt-to-SQL injections and the Toxic Agent Flow exploit in GitHub MCP servers. We analyze attack feasibility, review existing defenses, and discuss mitigation strategies such as dynamic trust management, cryptographic provenance tracking, and sandboxed agent interfaces. The framework is validated through expert review and cross-mapping with real-world incidents and public vulnerability repositories, including CVE and NIST NVD. Compared to prior surveys, this work presents the first integrated taxonomy bridging input-level exploits and protocol-layer vulnerabilities in LLM-agent ecosystems, offering actionable guidance for designing secure and resilient agentic AI systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The paper is published in ICT Express (Elsevier)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23260","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-07-01T02:41:48Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:41:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-16T04:46:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23046","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23046","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23046","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianzhe","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Xianzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuhui","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xuhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuanyang","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Chuanyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kolby","familyName":"Nottingham","name":"Nottingham, Kolby","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maarten","familyName":"Sap","name":"Sap, Maarten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SoMi-ToM: Evaluating Multi-Perspective Theory of Mind in Embodied Social Interactions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-29T00:54:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:34:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-30T11:52:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-01T00:57:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-15T04:45:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-16T02:13:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Humans continuously infer the states, goals, and behaviors of others by perceiving their surroundings in dynamic, real-world social interactions. However, most Theory of Mind (ToM) benchmarks only evaluate static, text-based scenarios, which have a significant gap compared to real interactions. We propose the SoMi-ToM benchmark, designed to evaluate multi-perspective ToM in embodied multi-agent complex social interactions. This benchmark is based on rich multimodal interaction data generated by the interaction environment SoMi, covering diverse crafting goals and social relationships. Our framework supports multi-level evaluation: (1) first-person evaluation provides multimodal (visual, dialogue, action, etc.) input from a first-person perspective during a task for real-time state inference, (2) third-person evaluation provides complete third-person perspective video and text records after a task for goal and behavior inference. This evaluation method allows for a more comprehensive examination of a model's ToM capabilities from both the subjective immediate experience and the objective global observation. We constructed a challenging dataset containing 35 third-person perspective videos, 363 first-person perspective images, and 1225 expert-annotated multiple-choice questions (three options). On this dataset, we systematically evaluated the performance of human subjects and several state-of-the-art large vision-language models (LVLMs). The results show that LVLMs perform significantly worse than humans on SoMi-ToM: the average accuracy gap between humans and models is 40.1% in first-person evaluation and 26.4% in third-person evaluation. This indicates that future LVLMs need to further improve their ToM capabilities in embodied, complex social interactions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23046","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:36:42Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:36:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-16T04:46:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23982","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23982","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23982","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiyang","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Ruiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Jing","name":"Jing, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Haibao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zaiqing","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Zaiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StyleDrive: Towards Driving-Style Aware Benchmarking of End-To-End Autonomous Driving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.4.9","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T15:48:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:27:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-03T09:28:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08-05T00:47:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T15:45:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-19T01:56:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Personalization, while extensively studied in conventional autonomous driving pipelines, has been largely overlooked in the context of end-to-end autonomous driving (E2EAD), despite its critical role in fostering user trust, safety perception, and real-world adoption. A primary bottleneck is the absence of large-scale real-world datasets that systematically capture driving preferences, severely limiting the development and evaluation of personalized E2EAD models. In this work, we introduce the first large-scale real-world dataset explicitly curated for personalized E2EAD, integrating comprehensive scene topology with rich dynamic context derived from agent dynamics and semantics inferred via a fine-tuned vision-language model (VLM). We propose a hybrid annotation pipeline that combines behavioral analysis, rule-and-distribution-based heuristics, and subjective semantic modeling guided by VLM reasoning, with final refinement through human-in-the-loop verification. Building upon this dataset, we introduce the first standardized benchmark for systematically evaluating personalized E2EAD models. Empirical evaluations on state-of-the-art architectures demonstrate that incorporating personalized driving preferences significantly improves behavioral alignment with human demonstrations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 7 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23982","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:59:03Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:59:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-19T03:15:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22242","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22242","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22242","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiahui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yurui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yurui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueming","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yueming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ze","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Ze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanpeng","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yanpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Jie","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Yu-Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyue","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Xinyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guowei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Guowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingyue","familyName":"Quan","name":"Quan, Xingyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"4D-VLA: Spatiotemporal Vision-Language-Action Pretraining with Cross-Scene Calibration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T14:09:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:39:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-18T14:43:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-19T01:53:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Leveraging diverse robotic data for pretraining remains a critical challenge. Existing methods typically model the dataset's action distribution using simple observations as inputs. However, these inputs are often incomplete, resulting in a dispersed conditional action distribution-an issue we refer to as coordinate system chaos and state chaos. This inconsistency significantly hampers pretraining efficiency. To address this, we propose 4D-VLA, a novel approach that effectively integrates 4D information into the input to mitigate these sources of chaos. Our model introduces depth and temporal information into visual features with sequential RGB-D inputs, aligning the coordinate systems of the robot and the scene. This alignment endows the model with strong spatiotemporal reasoning capabilities while minimizing training overhead. Additionally, we introduce memory bank sampling, a frame sampling strategy designed to extract informative frames from historical images, further improving effectiveness and efficiency. Experimental results demonstrate that our pretraining method and architectural components substantially enhance model performance. In both simulated and real-world experiments, our model achieves a significant increase in success rate over OpenVLA. To further assess spatial perception and generalization to novel views, we introduce MV-Bench, a multi-view simulation benchmark. Our model consistently outperforms existing methods, demonstrating stronger spatial understanding and adaptability."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22242","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:37:41Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:37:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-19T03:14:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.24086","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.24086","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.24086","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingfan","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Bingfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biao","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Biao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Sunyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shixiang","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Shixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linjie","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Linjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youyi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Youyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MotionGPT3: Human Motion as a Second Modality"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T17:42:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:32:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-20T03:37:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T01:07:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-03T02:15:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-04T02:11:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid progress of large language models (LLMs), multimodal frameworks that unify understanding and generation have become promising, yet they face increasing complexity as the number of modalities and tasks grows. We observe that motion quantization introduces approximation errors that cap motion quality, and that unifying discrete text and continuous motion within a single-stream backbone amplifies cross-modal interference. Motivated by recent multi-branch Transformer designs that separate signals from different modalities, we propose MotionGPT3, a bimodal motion-language model for both understanding and generation. MotionGPT3 encodes raw motion into a continuous latent space using a variational autoencoder (VAE), thereby avoiding quantization-induced artifacts, while leveraging the semantic prior of pretrained language models. A dual-stream Transformer with shared attention preserves modality-specific routes while enabling controlled, bidirectional information flow, which reduces interference, stabilizing optimization, and empirically accelerates convergence without degrading fidelity. For multimodal joint training, a generate-then-align three-stage schedule further improves stability and limits cross-task interference. Experiments show that MotionGPT3 achieves 2x faster convergence in training loss and up to 4x faster convergence in validation, while maintaining state-of-the-art performance on standard motion understanding and motion generation benchmarks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages, 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.24086","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T03:01:37Z","registered":"2025-07-01T03:01:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-04T04:52:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23068","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23068","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23068","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haoxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haifeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Faccio","name":"Faccio, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jürgen","familyName":"Schmidhuber","name":"Schmidhuber, Jürgen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengyue","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Mengyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-29T03:05:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:36:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-01T06:33:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08-04T00:22:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-26T03:16:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:49:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When building a world model, a common assumption is that the environment has a single, unchanging underlying causal rule, like applying Newton's laws to every situation. In reality, what appears as a drifting causal mechanism is often the manifestation of a fixed underlying mechanism seen through a narrow observational window. This brings about a problem that, when building a world model, even subtle shifts in policy or environment states can alter the very observed causal mechanisms. In this work, we introduce the \\textbf{Meta-Causal Graph} as world models, a minimal unified representation that efficiently encodes the transformation rules governing how causal structures shift across different latent world states. A single Meta-Causal Graph is composed of multiple causal subgraphs, each triggered by meta state, which is in the latent state space. Building on this representation, we introduce a \\textbf{Causality-Seeking Agent} whose objectives are to (1) identify the meta states that trigger each subgraph, (2) discover the corresponding causal relationships by agent curiosity-driven intervention policy, and (3) iteratively refine the Meta-Causal Graph through ongoing curiosity-driven exploration and agent experiences. Experiments on both synthetic tasks and a challenging robot arm manipulation task demonstrate that our method robustly captures shifts in causal dynamics and generalizes effectively to previously unseen contexts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"30 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23068","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:37:13Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:37:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:58:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23670","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23670","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23670","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammadmahdi","familyName":"Nouriborji","name":"Nouriborji, Mohammadmahdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Morteza","familyName":"Rohanian","name":"Rohanian, Morteza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Interleaved Speech Modeling through Knowledge Distillation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T09:47:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:09:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-21T18:45:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-23T00:04:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current speech language models exceed the size and latency constraints of many deployment environments. We build compact, expressive speech generation models through layer-aligned distillation, matching hidden states, attention maps, and softened logits to compress large multimodal transformers by 3x with minimal loss in performance. We introduce TinyWave, a family of 2B-parameter models for speech-to-speech and interleaved speech-text generation, trained on 50,000 hours of public audio. TinyWave supports (i) speech-only generation using phonetic or expressive tokens and (ii) mixed speech-text continuations. Evaluation on Libri-Light shows TinyWave within 1.4 normalized perplexity points of its teacher. Accuracy on spoken StoryCloze and SALMon reaches 93-97% of the teacher's performance, outperforming size-matched baselines. These models are optimized for deployment on commodity hardware, enabling applications in real-time conversational agents, assistive technologies, and low-resource environments. We release models, training code, and evaluation scripts to support reproducible research on compact, expressive speech generation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23670","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:51:38Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:51:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-23T02:14:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23434","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23434","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23434","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianran","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tianran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengwen","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Shengwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Rhinehart","name":"Rhinehart, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards foundational LiDAR world models with efficient latent flow matching"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T00:16:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:55:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-21T23:09:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-23T00:11:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LiDAR-based world models offer more structured and geometry-aware representations than their image-based counterparts. However, existing LiDAR world models are narrowly trained; each model excels only in the domain for which it was built. Can we develop LiDAR world models that exhibit strong transferability across multiple domains? We conduct the first systematic domain transfer study across three demanding scenarios: (i) outdoor to indoor generalization, (ii) sparse-beam \u0026amp; dense-beam adaptation, and (iii) non-semantic to semantic transfer. Given different amounts of fine-tuning data, our experiments show that a single pre-trained model can achieve up to 11% absolute improvement (83% relative) over training from scratch and outperforms training from scratch in 30/36 of our comparisons. This transferability of dynamic learning significantly reduces the reliance on manually annotated data for semantic occupancy forecasting: our method exceed the previous semantic occupancy forecasting models with only 5% of the labeled training data required by prior models. We also observed inefficiencies of current LiDAR world models, mainly through their under-compression of LiDAR data and inefficient training objectives. To address this, we propose a latent conditional flow matching (CFM)-based frameworks that achieves state-of-the-art reconstruction accuracy using only half the training data and a compression ratio 6 times higher than that of prior methods. Our model achieves SOTA performance on future-trajectory-conditioned semantic occupancy forecasting while being 23x more computationally efficient (a 28x FPS speedup); and achieves SOTA performance on semantic occupancy forecasting while being 2x more computationally efficient (a 1.1x FPS speedup)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the Thirty-Ninth Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025), 25 pages, 13 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23434","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:45:57Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:45:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-23T02:13:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.24000","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.24000","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.24000","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijun","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Lijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"He","name":"He, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zilei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zilei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tieniu","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Tieniu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Illusion of Progress? A Critical Look at Test-Time Adaptation for Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T16:05:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:28:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T13:09:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:55:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Test-time adaptation (TTA) methods have gained significant attention for enhancing the performance of vision-language models (VLMs) such as CLIP during inference, without requiring additional labeled data. However, current TTA researches generally suffer from major limitations such as duplication of baseline results, limited evaluation metrics, inconsistent experimental settings, and insufficient analysis. These problems hinder fair comparisons between TTA methods and make it difficult to assess their practical strengths and weaknesses. To address these challenges, we introduce TTA-VLM, a comprehensive benchmark for evaluating TTA methods on VLMs. Our benchmark implements 8 episodic TTA and 7 online TTA methods within a unified and reproducible framework, and evaluates them across 15 widely used datasets. Unlike prior studies focused solely on CLIP, we extend the evaluation to SigLIP--a model trained with a Sigmoid loss--and include training-time tuning methods such as CoOp, MaPLe, and TeCoA to assess generality. Beyond classification accuracy, TTA-VLM incorporates various evaluation metrics, including robustness, calibration, out-of-distribution detection, and stability, enabling a more holistic assessment of TTA methods. Through extensive experiments, we find that 1) existing TTA methods produce limited gains compared to the previous pioneering work; 2) current TTA methods exhibit poor collaboration with training-time fine-tuning methods; 3) accuracy gains frequently come at the cost of reduced model trustworthiness. We release TTA-VLM to provide fair comparison and comprehensive evaluation of TTA methods for VLMs, and we hope it encourages the community to develop more reliable and generalizable TTA strategies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025 Datasets and Benchmarks Track. Github link: https://github.com/TomSheng21/tta-vlm"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.24000","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:59:28Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:59:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:16:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.24120","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.24120","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.24120","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangding","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Shangding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Data Uniformity Improves Training Efficiency and More, with a Convergence Framework Beyond the NTK Regime"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T17:58:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:34:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-29T17:59:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-30T02:23:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Data selection plays a crucial role in data-driven decision-making, including in large language models (LLMs), and is typically task-dependent. Properties such as data quality and diversity have been extensively studied and are known to enhance model performance. However, it remains unclear whether there exist other quantitative and general principles of data selection that can consistently improve performance, especially for complicated tasks. In this paper, we demonstrate that selecting more uniformly distributed data can improve training efficiency while enhancing performance. Specifically, we establish that more uniform (less biased) distribution leads to a larger minimum pairwise distance between data points, denoted by $h_{\\min}$, and prove that a smaller $h_{\\min}$ can slow down the training dynamics of gradient descent (GD). Moreover, we theoretically show that the approximation error of neural networks decreases as $h_{\\min}$ increases. Our analysis introduces a convergence framework for GD beyond the Neural Tangent Kernel (NTK) regime, applicable to a broad class of architectures, including transformers, without requiring Lipschitz smoothness. This framework further provides theoretical justification for the use of residual connection and function composition in deep neural architectures. In the end, we conduct comprehensive experiments for supervised fine-tuning across various settings, including different optimization strategies, model sizes, and training datasets. The results consistently demonstrate that selecting data by maximizing pairwise distance significantly accelerates training and achieves comparable or better performance in LLMs across diverse datasets. Code and Datasets are available at the link: https://github.com/SafeRL-Lab/data-uniformity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.24120","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T03:02:25Z","registered":"2025-07-01T03:02:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-30T05:22:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23601","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23601","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23601","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijie","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Weijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaguang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yaguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingzhi","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Jingzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shibo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengze","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Mengze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sirui","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Sirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajie","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jiajie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiaofang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Semantic-guided Diverse Decoding for Large Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T08:06:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:05:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-28T21:32:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-30T01:21:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diverse decoding of large language models is crucial for applications requiring multiple semantically distinct responses, yet existing methods primarily achieve lexical rather than semantic diversity. This limitation significantly constrains Best-of-N strategies, group-based reinforcement learning, and data synthesis. While temperature sampling and diverse beam search modify token distributions or apply n-gram penalties, they fail to ensure meaningful semantic differentiation. We introduce Semantic-guided Diverse Decoding (SemDiD), operating directly in embedding space that balances quality with diversity through three complementary mechanisms: orthogonal directional guidance, dynamic inter-group repulsion, and position-debiased probability assessment. SemDiD harmonizes these competing objectives using adaptive gain functions and constraint optimization, ensuring both quality thresholds and maximal semantic differentiation. Experiments show SemDiD consistently outperforms existing methods, improving Best-of-N coverage by 1.4-5.2% across diverse tasks and accelerating RLHF training convergence by 15% while increasing accuracy by up to 2.1%."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23601","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:49:58Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:49:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-30T05:17:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23906","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23906","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23906","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aleksandros","familyName":"Sobczyk","name":"Sobczyk, Aleksandros","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe","familyName":"Sorrentino","name":"Sorrentino, Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anastasios","familyName":"Zouzias","name":"Zouzias, Anastasios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Segmented Operations using Matrix Multiplications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Complexity (cs.CC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T14:36:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:22:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T15:52:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:58:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Specialized computational units that perform small matrix multiplications as primitive operations are typically present in modern AI accelerators. However, these Matrix Multiplication Units (MMUs) are often underutilized for many fundamental deep learning operations besides dense matrix multiplications. Coincidentally, the lack of a rigorous theoretical model of computation for such architectures obstructs algorithmic design. In this work, we propose MMV-RAM, a computational model which judiciously extends the Vector-RAM model with an additional MMU. We provide a detailed theoretical analysis and carefully balance the computational power between the matrix and vector units, guided by the circuit complexity lower bound that parity is not in AC{[0]}. Given MMV-RAM, we proceed to algorithm design, starting with two fundamental parallel operations: segmented scan and sum. By expressing them as compositions of elementary parallel primitives (e.g., seg. sum reduces to: scan, compress, and vector differentiation), we can exploit MMUs to perform speculative blocked computations, ultimately leading to provable theoretical speed-ups against vector-only approaches. These results extend to other ubiquitous AI kernels, including dense matrix product, and sparse matrix-vector product. As a case study, we implemented the proposed algorithms on the Ascend 910B AI accelerator, which contains matrix and vector cores. We evaluate these implementations on synthetic and real-world datasets from various applications, including Large Language Models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23906","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:57:15Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:57:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-25T02:28:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23126","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23126","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23126","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suning","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Suning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianzhong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Qianzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiankai","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jiankai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mac","familyName":"Schwager","name":"Schwager, Mac","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ParticleFormer: A 3D Point Cloud World Model for Multi-Object, Multi-Material Robotic Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-29T07:23:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:39:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T16:38:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-02T00:53:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-04T07:47:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07-08T00:25:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-08-25T18:24:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-08-27T00:03:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"3D world models (i.e., learning-based 3D dynamics models) offer a promising approach to generalizable robotic manipulation by capturing the underlying physics of environment evolution conditioned on robot actions. However, existing 3D world models are primarily limited to single-material dynamics using a particle-based Graph Neural Network model, and often require time-consuming 3D scene reconstruction to obtain 3D particle tracks for training. In this work, we present ParticleFormer, a Transformer-based point cloud world model trained with a hybrid point cloud reconstruction loss, supervising both global and local dynamics features in multi-material, multi-object robot interactions. ParticleFormer captures fine-grained multi-object interactions between rigid, deformable, and flexible materials, trained directly from real-world robot perception data without an elaborate scene reconstruction. We demonstrate the model's effectiveness both in 3D scene forecasting tasks, and in downstream manipulation tasks using a Model Predictive Control (MPC) policy. In addition, we extend existing dynamics learning benchmarks to include diverse multi-material, multi-object interaction scenarios. We validate our method on six simulation and three real-world experiments, where it consistently outperforms leading baselines by achieving superior dynamics prediction accuracy and less rollout error in downstream visuomotor tasks. Experimental videos are available at https://suninghuang19.github.io/particleformer_page/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23126","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:38:37Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:38:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-08-27T02:04:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22557","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22557","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22557","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Boyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghao","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Minghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdul","familyName":"Basit","name":"Basit, Abdul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siddharth","familyName":"Garg","name":"Garg, Siddharth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad","familyName":"Shafique","name":"Shafique, Muhammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MetaCipher: A Time-Persistent and Universal Multi-Agent Framework for Cipher-Based Jailbreak Attacks for LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T18:15:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:03:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-13T10:28:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08-14T00:34:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) grow more capable, they face growing vulnerability to sophisticated jailbreak attacks. While developers invest heavily in alignment finetuning and safety guardrails, researchers continue publishing novel attacks, driving progress through adversarial iteration. This dynamic mirrors a strategic game of continual evolution. However, two major challenges hinder jailbreak development: the high cost of querying top-tier LLMs and the short lifespan of effective attacks due to frequent safety updates. These factors limit cost-efficiency and practical impact of research in jailbreak attacks. To address this, we propose MetaCipher, a low-cost, multi-agent jailbreak framework that generalizes across LLMs with varying safety measures. Using reinforcement learning, MetaCipher is modular and adaptive, supporting extensibility to future strategies. Within as few as 10 queries, MetaCipher achieves state-of-the-art attack success rates on recent malicious prompt benchmarks, outperforming prior jailbreak methods. We conduct a large-scale empirical evaluation across diverse victim models and benchmarks, demonstrating its robustness and adaptability. Warning: This paper contains model outputs that may be offensive or harmful, shown solely to demonstrate jailbreak efficacy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22557","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:25:03Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:25:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-08-14T01:59:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.24006","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.24006","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.24006","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anselm R.","familyName":"Strohmaier","name":"Strohmaier, Anselm R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wim","familyName":"Van Dooren","name":"Van Dooren, Wim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kathrin","familyName":"Seßler","name":"Seßler, Kathrin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Greer","name":"Greer, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lieven","familyName":"Verschaffel","name":"Verschaffel, Lieven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Large Language Models Don't Make Sense of Word Problems. A Scoping Review from a Mathematics Education Perspective"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"History and Overview (math.HO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T16:10:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:28:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-09T21:42:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08-12T00:41:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The progress of Large Language Models (LLMs) like ChatGPT raises the question of how they can be integrated into education. One hope is that they can support mathematics learning, including word-problem solving. Since LLMs can handle textual input with ease, they appear well-suited for solving mathematical word problems. Yet their real competence, whether they can make sense of the real-world context, and the implications for classrooms remain unclear. We conducted a scoping review from a mathematics-education perspective, including three parts: a technical overview, a systematic review of word problems used in research, and a state-of-the-art empirical evaluation of LLMs on mathematical word problems. First, in the technical overview, we contrast the conceptualization of word problems and their solution processes between LLMs and students. In computer-science research this is typically labeled mathematical reasoning, a term that does not align with usage in mathematics education. Second, our literature review of 213 studies shows that the most popular word-problem corpora are dominated by s-problems, which do not require a consideration of realities of their real-world context. Finally, our evaluation of GPT-3.5-turbo, GPT-4o-mini, GPT-4.1, o3, and GPT-5 on 287 word problems shows that most recent LLMs solve these s-problems with near-perfect accuracy, including a perfect score on 20 problems from PISA. LLMs still showed weaknesses in tackling problems where the real-world context is problematic or non-sensical. In sum, we argue based on all three aspects that LLMs have mastered a superficial solution process but do not make sense of word problems, which potentially limits their value as instructional tools in mathematics classrooms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"v2: added analyses for GPT-5, also leading to small adjustments in the text, no major new interpretations"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.24006","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:59:37Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:59:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-08-12T03:32:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23825","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23825","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23825","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoji","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haoji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yansong","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yansong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiashi","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Jiashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojie","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Xiaojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Flash-VStream: Efficient Real-Time Understanding for Long Video Streams"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T13:17:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:19:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-24T07:25:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-25T00:22:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Benefiting from the advances in large language models and cross-modal alignment, existing multimodal large language models have achieved prominent performance in image and short video understanding. However, the understanding of long videos is still challenging, as their long-context nature results in significant computational and memory overhead. Most existing work treats long videos in the same way as short videos, which is inefficient for real-world applications and hard to generalize to even longer videos. To address these issues, we propose Flash-VStream, an efficient video language model capable of processing extremely long videos and responding to user queries in real time. Particularly, we design a Flash Memory module, containing a low-capacity context memory to aggregate long-context temporal information and model the distribution of information density, and a high-capacity augmentation memory to retrieve detailed spatial information based on this distribution. Compared to existing models, Flash-VStream achieves significant reductions in inference latency. Extensive experiments on long video benchmarks and comprehensive video benchmarks, i.e., EgoSchema, MLVU, LVBench, MVBench and Video-MME, demonstrate the state-of-the-art performance and outstanding efficiency of our method. Code is available at https://github.com/IVGSZ/Flash-VStream."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23825","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:55:20Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:55:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-25T01:54:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23276","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23276","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23276","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David Guzman","familyName":"Piedrahita","name":"Piedrahita, David Guzman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongjin","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yongjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mrinmaya","familyName":"Sachan","name":"Sachan, Mrinmaya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giorgia","familyName":"Ramponi","name":"Ramponi, Giorgia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernhard","familyName":"Schölkopf","name":"Schölkopf, Bernhard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijing","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Zhijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Corrupted by Reasoning: Reasoning Language Models Become Free-Riders in Public Goods Games"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-29T15:02:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:46:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-24T13:13:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-25T00:38:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed as autonomous agents, understanding their cooperation and social mechanisms is becoming increasingly important. In particular, how LLMs balance self-interest and collective well-being is a critical challenge for ensuring alignment, robustness, and safe deployment. In this paper, we examine the challenge of costly sanctioning in multi-agent LLM systems, where an agent must decide whether to invest its own resources to incentivize cooperation or penalize defection. To study this, we adapt a public goods game with institutional choice from behavioral economics, allowing us to observe how different LLMs navigate social dilemmas over repeated interactions. Our analysis reveals four distinct behavioral patterns among models: some consistently establish and sustain high levels of cooperation, others fluctuate between engagement and disengagement, some gradually decline in cooperative behavior over time, and others rigidly follow fixed strategies regardless of outcomes. Surprisingly, we find that reasoning LLMs, such as the o1 series, struggle significantly with cooperation, whereas some traditional LLMs consistently achieve high levels of cooperation. These findings suggest that the current approach to improving LLMs, which focuses on enhancing their reasoning capabilities, does not necessarily lead to cooperation, providing valuable insights for deploying LLM agents in environments that require sustained collaboration. Our code is available at https://github.com/davidguzmanp/SanctSim"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at COLM 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23276","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:42:10Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:42:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-25T01:54:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23468","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23468","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23468","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyu","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Junyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changsheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Changsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NavMorph: A Self-Evolving World Model for Vision-and-Language Navigation in Continuous Environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T02:20:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:57:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-22T03:03:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-23T00:15:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-and-Language Navigation in Continuous Environments (VLN-CE) requires agents to execute sequential navigation actions in complex environments guided by natural language instructions. Current approaches often struggle with generalizing to novel environments and adapting to ongoing changes during navigation. Inspired by human cognition, we present NavMorph, a self-evolving world model framework that enhances environmental understanding and decision-making in VLN-CE tasks. NavMorph employs compact latent representations to model environmental dynamics, equipping agents with foresight for adaptive planning and policy refinement. By integrating a novel Contextual Evolution Memory, NavMorph leverages scene-contextual information to support effective navigation while maintaining online adaptability. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method achieves notable performance improvements on popular VLN-CE benchmarks. Code is available at https://github.com/Feliciaxyao/NavMorph."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23468","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:46:46Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:46:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-23T02:09:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23491","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23491","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23491","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"ZongHan","familyName":"Hsieh","name":"Hsieh, ZongHan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tzer-Jen","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Tzer-Jen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"ShengJing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, ShengJing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ZonUI-3B: A Lightweight Vision-Language Model for Cross-Resolution GUI Grounding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T03:33:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:59:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T08:46:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-02T00:31:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-18T06:03:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07-21T00:18:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we present ZonUI-3B, a lightweight Vision-Language Model (VLM) that can be fully trained on a single consumer-grade GPU (RTX 4090) while delivering performance comparable to significantly larger models on GUI grounding tasks. The model incorporates several key innovations: (i) combine cross-platform, multi-resolution dataset of 24K examples from diverse sources including mobile, desktop, and web GUI screenshots to effectively address data scarcity in high-resolution desktop environments; (ii) a two-stage fine-tuning strategy, where initial cross-platform training establishes robust GUI understanding, followed by specialized fine-tuning on high-resolution data to significantly enhance model adaptability; and (iii) data curation and redundancy reduction strategies, demonstrating that randomly sampling a smaller subset with reduced redundancy achieves performance comparable to larger datasets, emphasizing data diversity over sheer volume. Empirical evaluation on standard GUI grounding benchmarks, including ScreenSpot, ScreenSpot-v2, and the challenging ScreenSpot-Pro, highlights ZonUI-3B's exceptional accuracy, achieving 84.9% on ScreenSpot and 86.4% on ScreenSpot-v2, surpassing prior models under 4B parameters. Ablation studies validate the critical role of balanced sampling and two-stage fine-tuning in enhancing robustness, particularly in high-resolution desktop scenarios. The ZonUI-3B is available at: https://github.com/Han1018/ZonUI-3B"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23491","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:47:19Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:47:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-21T01:43:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23603","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23603","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23603","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baihe","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Baihe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanna","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yanna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangsheng","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Guangsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caijun","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Caijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuelei","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Xuelei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"He","name":"He, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ren Ping","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ren Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SoK: Semantic Privacy in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T08:08:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:05:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-16T15:51:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-17T00:49:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in sensitive domains, traditional data privacy measures prove inadequate for protecting information that is implicit, contextual, or inferable - what we define as semantic privacy. This Systematization of Knowledge (SoK) introduces a lifecycle-centric framework to analyze how semantic privacy risks emerge across input processing, pretraining, fine-tuning, and alignment stages of LLMs. We categorize key attack vectors and assess how current defenses, such as differential privacy, embedding encryption, edge computing, and unlearning, address these threats. Our analysis reveals critical gaps in semantic-level protection, especially against contextual inference and latent representation leakage. We conclude by outlining open challenges, including quantifying semantic leakage, protecting multimodal inputs, balancing de-identification with generation quality, and ensuring transparency in privacy enforcement. This work aims to inform future research on designing robust, semantically aware privacy-preserving techniques for LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23603","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:50:00Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:50:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-17T02:04:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22853","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22853","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22853","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyochul","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Kyochul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donghyeon","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Donghyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyusik","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Kyusik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongseok","familyName":"Heo","name":"Heo, Dongseok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taewhoo","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Taewhoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Woojeong","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Woojeong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bongwon","familyName":"Suh","name":"Suh, Bongwon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DICE-BENCH: Evaluating the Tool-Use Capabilities of Large Language Models in Multi-Round, Multi-Party Dialogues"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-28T11:28:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:23:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-02T07:55:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-03T00:26:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing function-calling benchmarks focus on single-turn interactions. However, they overlook the complexity of real-world scenarios. To quantify how existing benchmarks address practical applications, we introduce DICE-SCORE, a metric that evaluates the dispersion of tool-related information such as function name and parameter values throughout the dialogue. Analyzing existing benchmarks through DICE-SCORE reveals notably low scores, highlighting the need for more realistic scenarios. To address this gap, we present DICE-BENCH, a framework that constructs practical function-calling datasets by synthesizing conversations through a tool graph that maintains dependencies across rounds and a multi-agent system with distinct personas to enhance dialogue naturalness. The final dataset comprises 1,607 high-DICE-SCORE instances. Our experiments on 19 LLMs with DICE-BENCH show that significant advances are still required before such models can be deployed effectively in real-world settings. Our code and data are all publicly available: https://snuhcc.github.io/DICE-Bench/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, ACL 2025 Vienna"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22853","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:32:08Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:32:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-03T02:05:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23940","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23940","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23940","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianxiang","familyName":"An","name":"An, Jianxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianwei","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Tianwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyang","familyName":"He","name":"He, Hongyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongzhe","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Hongzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenqiao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wenqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheqi","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Zheqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siliang","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Siliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueting","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Yueting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Graft: Integrating the Domain Knowledge via Efficient Parameter Synergy for MLLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T15:07:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:24:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T03:26:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-02T00:20:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have achieved success across various domains. However, their applicability tends to degrade when confronted with different types of data inputs, especially for MLLMs that have been fine-tuned for specific tasks. Despite its importance, the study of knowledge sharing among domain-specific MLLMs--such as those trained for mathematics or code--remains largely underexplored. To address the fragmentation of knowledge across domain-specialized MLLMs, we propose a unified parameter integration framework that enables modular composition of expert capabilities. Our method is grounded in a novel Compatibility-Aware Parameter Splicing (CAPS) strategy, which leverages both local functional attribution and global information-theoretic signals to guide selective parameter fusion. By extending this mechanism to the low-rank adaptation layer granularity, we ensure efficient integration with minimal inference overhead. Furthermore, we introduce a domain compatibility scoring mechanism that quantifies inter-expert alignment at the activation level and correlates with downstream task utility. This principled fusion protocol allows the final model to synergize heterogeneous expertise while preserving structural modularity. Extensive evaluations across diverse multimodal benchmarks validate the effectiveness of our framework, offering a scalable path toward compositional, domain-adaptive MLLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23940","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:58:03Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:58:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-02T02:06:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23743","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23743","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23743","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiziano","familyName":"Labruna","name":"Labruna, Tiziano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone","familyName":"Gallo","name":"Gallo, Simone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giovanni Da San","familyName":"Martino","name":"Martino, Giovanni Da San","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Positional Bias in Binary Question Answering: How Uncertainty Shapes Model Preferences"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T11:30:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:14:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T10:39:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-02T00:36:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Positional bias in binary question answering occurs when a model systematically favors one choice over another based solely on the ordering of presented options. In this study, we quantify and analyze positional bias across five large language models under varying degrees of answer uncertainty. We re-adapted the SQuAD-it dataset by adding an extra incorrect answer option and then created multiple versions with progressively less context and more out-of-context answers, yielding datasets that range from low to high uncertainty. Additionally, we evaluate two naturally higher-uncertainty benchmarks: (1) WebGPT - question pairs with unequal human-assigned quality scores, and (2) Winning Arguments - where models predict the more persuasive argument in Reddit's r/ChangeMyView exchanges. Across each dataset, the order of the \"correct\" (or higher-quality/persuasive) option is systematically flipped (first placed in position 1, then in position 2) to compute both Preference Fairness and Position Consistency. We observe that positional bias is nearly absent under low-uncertainty conditions, but grows exponentially when it becomes doubtful to decide which option is correct."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23743","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:53:24Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:53:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-02T02:06:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22419","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22419","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22419","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingchen","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Bingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Despoina","familyName":"Magka","name":"Magka, Despoina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minqi","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Minqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xian","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roberta","familyName":"Raileanu","name":"Raileanu, Roberta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatiana","familyName":"Shavrina","name":"Shavrina, Tatiana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jean-Christophe","familyName":"Gagnon-Audet","name":"Gagnon-Audet, Jean-Christophe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kelvin","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Kelvin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shagun","familyName":"Sodhani","name":"Sodhani, Shagun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Shvartsman","name":"Shvartsman, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrei","familyName":"Lupu","name":"Lupu, Andrei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alisia","familyName":"Lupidi","name":"Lupidi, Alisia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edan","familyName":"Toledo","name":"Toledo, Edan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karen","familyName":"Hambardzumyan","name":"Hambardzumyan, Karen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Josifoski","name":"Josifoski, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Foster","name":"Foster, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucia","familyName":"Cipolina-Kun","name":"Cipolina-Kun, Lucia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhishek","familyName":"Charnalia","name":"Charnalia, Abhishek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Derek","familyName":"Dunfield","name":"Dunfield, Derek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander H.","familyName":"Miller","name":"Miller, Alexander H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oisin","familyName":"Mac Aodha","name":"Mac Aodha, Oisin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakob","familyName":"Foerster","name":"Foerster, Jakob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoram","familyName":"Bachrach","name":"Bachrach, Yoram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Automated LLM Speedrunning Benchmark: Reproducing NanoGPT Improvements"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T17:44:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:49:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T21:56:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-02T00:11:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Rapid advancements in large language models (LLMs) have the potential to assist in scientific progress. A critical capability toward this endeavor is the ability to reproduce existing work. To evaluate the ability of AI agents to reproduce results in an active research area, we introduce the Automated LLM Speedrunning Benchmark, leveraging the research community contributions on the NanoGPT speedrun, a competition to train a GPT-2 model in the shortest time. Each of the 19 speedrun tasks provides the agent with the previous records training script, optionally paired with one of three hint formats, ranging from pseudocode to paper-like descriptions of the new records improvements. Records execute quickly by design and speedrun improvements encompass diverse code-level changes, ranging from high-level algorithmic advancements to hardware-aware optimizations. These features make the benchmark both accessible and realistic for the frontier problem of improving LLM training. We find that recent reasoning LLMs combined with SoTA scaffolds struggle to reimplement already-known innovations in our benchmark, even when given detailed hints. Our benchmark thus provides a simple, non-saturated measure of an LLMs ability to automate scientific reproduction, a necessary (but not sufficient) skill for an autonomous research agent."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22419","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:41:52Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:41:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-02T02:05:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.24113","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.24113","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.24113","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiwen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kaiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaotao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xiaotao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingang","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Xingang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyang","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Xiaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingwei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jingwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao-Xiao","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Xiao-Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xun","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Xun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Epona: Autoregressive Diffusion World Model for Autonomous Driving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T17:56:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:34:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have demonstrated exceptional visual quality in video generation, making them promising for autonomous driving world modeling. However, existing video diffusion-based world models struggle with flexible-length, long-horizon predictions and integrating trajectory planning. This is because conventional video diffusion models rely on global joint distribution modeling of fixed-length frame sequences rather than sequentially constructing localized distributions at each timestep. In this work, we propose Epona, an autoregressive diffusion world model that enables localized spatiotemporal distribution modeling through two key innovations: 1) Decoupled spatiotemporal factorization that separates temporal dynamics modeling from fine-grained future world generation, and 2) Modular trajectory and video prediction that seamlessly integrate motion planning with visual modeling in an end-to-end framework. Our architecture enables high-resolution, long-duration generation while introducing a novel chain-of-forward training strategy to address error accumulation in autoregressive loops. Experimental results demonstrate state-of-the-art performance with 7.4\\% FVD improvement and minutes longer prediction duration compared to prior works. The learned world model further serves as a real-time motion planner, outperforming strong end-to-end planners on NAVSIM benchmarks. Code will be publicly available at \\href{https://github.com/Kevin-thu/Epona/}{https://github.com/Kevin-thu/Epona/}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICCV2025, Project Page: https://kevin-thu.github.io/Epona/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.24113","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T03:02:15Z","registered":"2025-07-01T03:02:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T03:02:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.24019","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.24019","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.24019","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongxin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hongxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zheyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zeyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zunzhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zunzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lixing","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Lixing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinhong","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Qinhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuang","familyName":"Gan","name":"Gan, Chuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Ella: Embodied Social Agents with Lifelong Memory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T16:22:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:28:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Ella, an embodied social agent capable of lifelong learning within a community in a 3D open world, where agents accumulate experiences and acquire knowledge through everyday visual observations and social interactions. At the core of Ella's capabilities is a structured, long-term multimodal memory system that stores, updates, and retrieves information effectively. It consists of a name-centric semantic memory for organizing acquired knowledge and a spatiotemporal episodic memory for capturing multimodal experiences. By integrating this lifelong memory system with foundation models, Ella retrieves relevant information for decision-making, plans daily activities, builds social relationships, and evolves autonomously while coexisting with other intelligent beings in the open world. We conduct capability-oriented evaluations in a dynamic 3D open world where 15 agents engage in social activities for days and are assessed with a suite of unseen controlled evaluations. Experimental results show that Ella can influence, lead, and cooperate with other agents well to achieve goals, showcasing its ability to learn effectively through observation and social interaction. Our findings highlight the transformative potential of combining structured memory systems with foundation models for advancing embodied intelligence. More videos can be found at https://umass-embodied-agi.github.io/Ella/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.24019","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:59:56Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:59:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:59:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.24016","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.24016","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.24016","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunjong","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyunjong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangyeop","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sangyeop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jongheon","familyName":"Jeong","name":"Jeong, Jongheon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeongjae","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Yeongjae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungzoon","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Sungzoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EXPERT: An Explainable Image Captioning Evaluation Metric with Structured Explanations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T16:20:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:28:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models and vision-language models have led to growing interest in explainable evaluation metrics for image captioning. However, these metrics generate explanations without standardized criteria, and the overall quality of the generated explanations remains unverified. In this paper, we propose EXPERT, a reference-free evaluation metric that provides structured explanations based on three fundamental criteria: fluency, relevance, and descriptiveness. By constructing large-scale datasets of high-quality structured explanations, we develop a two-stage evaluation template to effectively supervise a vision-language model for both scoring and explanation generation. EXPERT achieves state-of-the-art results on benchmark datasets while providing significantly higher-quality explanations than existing metrics, as validated through comprehensive human evaluation. Our code and datasets are available at https://github.com/hjkim811/EXPERT."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ACL 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.24016","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:59:52Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:59:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:59:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23930","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23930","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23930","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Bangladesh Institute of Governance,Management"],"givenName":"Ruhina Tabasshum","familyName":"Prome","name":"Prome, Ruhina Tabasshum","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["George Mason University"],"givenName":"Tarikul Islam","familyName":"Tamiti","name":"Tamiti, Tarikul Islam","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["George Mason University"],"givenName":"Anomadarshi","familyName":"Barua","name":"Barua, Anomadarshi","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Leveraging the Potential of Prompt Engineering for Hate Speech Detection in Low-Resource Languages"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T14:59:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:24:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid expansion of social media leads to a marked increase in hate speech, which threatens personal lives and results in numerous hate crimes. Detecting hate speech presents several challenges: diverse dialects, frequent code-mixing, and the prevalence of misspelled words in user-generated content on social media platforms. Recent progress in hate speech detection is typically concentrated on high-resource languages. However, low-resource languages still face significant challenges due to the lack of large-scale, high-quality datasets. This paper investigates how we can overcome this limitation via prompt engineering on large language models (LLMs) focusing on low-resource Bengali language. We investigate six prompting strategies - zero-shot prompting, refusal suppression, flattering the classifier, multi-shot prompting, role prompting, and finally our innovative metaphor prompting to detect hate speech effectively in low-resource languages. We pioneer the metaphor prompting to circumvent the built-in safety mechanisms of LLMs that marks a significant departure from existing jailbreaking methods. We investigate all six different prompting strategies on the Llama2-7B model and compare the results extensively with three pre-trained word embeddings - GloVe, Word2Vec, and FastText for three different deep learning models - multilayer perceptron (MLP), convolutional neural network (CNN), and bidirectional gated recurrent unit (BiGRU). To prove the effectiveness of our metaphor prompting in the low-resource Bengali language, we also evaluate it in another low-resource language - Hindi, and two high-resource languages - English and German. The performance of all prompting techniques is evaluated using the F1 score, and environmental impact factor (IF), which measures CO$_2$ emissions, electricity usage, and computational time."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23930","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:57:49Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:57:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:57:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23929","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23929","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23929","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed J.","familyName":"Saeed","name":"Saeed, Mohammed J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tommi","familyName":"Vehvilainen","name":"Vehvilainen, Tommi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evgeny","familyName":"Fedoseev","name":"Fedoseev, Evgeny","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sevil","familyName":"Caliskan","name":"Caliskan, Sevil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatiana","familyName":"Vodolazova","name":"Vodolazova, Tatiana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"IMPACT: Inflectional Morphology Probes Across Complex Typologies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T14:58:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:24:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown significant progress on various multilingual benchmarks and are increasingly used to generate and evaluate text in non-English languages. However, while they may produce fluent outputs, it remains unclear to what extent these models truly grasp the underlying linguistic complexity of those languages, particularly in morphology. To investigate this, we introduce IMPACT, a synthetically generated evaluation framework focused on inflectional morphology, which we publicly release, designed to evaluate LLM performance across five morphologically rich languages: Arabic, Russian, Finnish, Turkish, and Hebrew. IMPACT includes unit-test-style cases covering both shared and language-specific phenomena, from basic verb inflections (e.g., tense, number, gender) to unique features like Arabic's reverse gender agreement and vowel harmony in Finnish and Turkish. We assess eight multilingual LLMs that, despite strong English performance, struggle with other languages and uncommon morphological patterns, especially when judging ungrammatical examples. We also show that Chain of Thought and Thinking Models can degrade performance. Our work exposes gaps in LLMs' handling of linguistic complexity, pointing to clear room for improvement. To support further research, we publicly release the IMPACT framework."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23929","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:57:47Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:57:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:57:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23864","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23864","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23864","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seyed Mahed","familyName":"Mousavi","name":"Mousavi, Seyed Mahed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edoardo","familyName":"Cecchinato","name":"Cecchinato, Edoardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucia","familyName":"Hornikova","name":"Hornikova, Lucia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe","familyName":"Riccardi","name":"Riccardi, Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Garbage In, Reasoning Out? Why Benchmark Scores are Unreliable and What to Do About It"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T13:57:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:21:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We conduct a systematic audit of three widely used reasoning benchmarks, SocialIQa, FauxPas-EAI, and ToMi, and uncover pervasive flaws in both benchmark items and evaluation methodology. Using five LLMs (GPT-{3, 3.5, 4, o1}, and LLaMA 3.1) as diagnostic tools, we identify structural, semantic, and pragmatic issues in benchmark design (e.g., duplicated items, ambiguous wording, and implausible answers), as well as scoring procedures that prioritize output form over reasoning process. Through systematic human annotation and re-evaluation on cleaned benchmark subsets, we find that model scores often improve not due to due to erratic surface wording variations and not to improved reasoning. Infact, further analyses show that model performance is highly sensitive to minor input variations such as context availability and phrasing, revealing that high scores may reflect alignment with format-specific cues rather than consistent inference based on the input. These findings challenge the validity of current benchmark-based claims about reasoning in LLMs, and highlight the need for evaluation protocols that assess reasoning as a process of drawing inference from available information, rather than as static output selection. We release audited data and evaluation tools to support more interpretable and diagnostic assessments of model reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23864","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:56:16Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:56:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:56:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23856","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23856","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23856","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shihan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Shihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lianli","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Lianli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingkuan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Jingkuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicu","familyName":"Sebe","name":"Sebe, Nicu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng Tao","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Heng Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Closer Look at Conditional Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T13:51:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:21:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite the great promise of Prompt Tuning (PT) in adapting large Vision-Language Pretrained Models (VLPMs) to downstream tasks, they often struggle to overcome the Base-New Tradeoff (BNT) dilemma: as VLPMs are better tuned to a base task, their ability to generalize to new tasks diminishes. Recent work on conditional PT addresses this problem by replacing static prompts with dynamic Visual Image Information (VII)-conditioned prompts, improving the model's generalization to new tasks to some extent. In this work, we first identify a critical issue with existing conditional PT methods: using VII as the \"condition\" of prompts yields suboptimal performance, and even random noise-conditioned prompts can outperform the VII-conditioned counterparts. On further analysis, we find that learning dynamic prompts conditioned on Textual Class Information (TCI) is the key to solving the BNT problem. Motivated by this, we then propose Class-adaptive Prompt Tuning (CaPT), which enables fast adaptation of tuned models to new classes by learning TCI-conditioned prompts from base classes. Remarkably, CaPT can be used as a plugin to mitigate the BNT problem for existing unconditional PT schemes. Extensive experiments on 11 datasets show that CaPT consistently improves the performance of five strong unconditional PT baselines with negligible additional computational cost. Additionally, by integrating CaPT with our recently proposed DePT framework, we devise a new conditional PT approach, termed DeCaPT, which outperforms the H ACC of the state-of-the-art conditional PT scheme by 3.49%, averaged over the 11 datasets. Code: https://github.com/Koorye/CaPT."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23856","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:56:05Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:56:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:56:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23844","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23844","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23844","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinpeng","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Yinpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Survey on Autonomy-Induced Security Risks in Large Model-Based Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T13:34:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:20:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have catalyzed the rise of autonomous AI agents capable of perceiving, reasoning, and acting in dynamic, open-ended environments. These large-model agents mark a paradigm shift from static inference systems to interactive, memory-augmented entities. While these capabilities significantly expand the functional scope of AI, they also introduce qualitatively novel security risks - such as memory poisoning, tool misuse, reward hacking, and emergent misalignment - that extend beyond the threat models of conventional systems or standalone LLMs. In this survey, we first examine the structural foundations and key capabilities that underpin increasing levels of agent autonomy, including long-term memory retention, modular tool use, recursive planning, and reflective reasoning. We then analyze the corresponding security vulnerabilities across the agent stack, identifying failure modes such as deferred decision hazards, irreversible tool chains, and deceptive behaviors arising from internal state drift or value misalignment. These risks are traced to architectural fragilities that emerge across perception, cognition, memory, and action modules. To address these challenges, we systematically review recent defense strategies deployed at different autonomy layers, including input sanitization, memory lifecycle control, constrained decision-making, structured tool invocation, and introspective reflection. We introduce the Reflective Risk-Aware Agent Architecture (R2A2), a unified cognitive framework grounded in Constrained Markov Decision Processes (CMDPs), which incorporates risk-aware world modeling, meta-policy adaptation, and joint reward-risk optimization to enable principled, proactive safety across the agent's decision-making loop."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23844","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:55:48Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:55:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:55:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23785","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23785","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23785","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongjian","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yongjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiya","familyName":"Saiyin","name":"Saiyin, Jiya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingzheng","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Bingzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Visual Textualization for Image Prompted Object Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T12:27:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:16:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose VisTex-OVLM, a novel image prompted object detection method that introduces visual textualization -- a process that projects a few visual exemplars into the text feature space to enhance Object-level Vision-Language Models' (OVLMs) capability in detecting rare categories that are difficult to describe textually and nearly absent from their pre-training data, while preserving their pre-trained object-text alignment. Specifically, VisTex-OVLM leverages multi-scale textualizing blocks and a multi-stage fusion strategy to integrate visual information from visual exemplars, generating textualized visual tokens that effectively guide OVLMs alongside text prompts. Unlike previous methods, our method maintains the original architecture of OVLM, maintaining its generalization capabilities while enhancing performance in few-shot settings. VisTex-OVLM demonstrates superior performance across open-set datasets which have minimal overlap with OVLM's pre-training data and achieves state-of-the-art results on few-shot benchmarks PASCAL VOC and MSCOCO. The code will be released at https://github.com/WitGotFlg/VisTex-OVLM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23785","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:54:23Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:54:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:54:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23735","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23735","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23735","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"JiaRu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, JiaRu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingwei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mingwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AutoEvoEval: An Automated Framework for Evolving Close-Ended LLM Evaluation Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T11:18:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:13:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have shown remarkable performance on various tasks, but existing evaluation benchmarks are often static and insufficient to fully assess their robustness and generalization in realistic scenarios. Prior work using evolutionary or adversarial data augmentation has improved evaluation diversity but lacks systematic control over perturbation types and multi-step complexity, limiting comprehensive robustness analysis. To address these gaps, we propose AutoEvoEval, an evolution-based evaluation framework for close-ended tasks such as multi-choice question answering. AutoEvoEval introduces 22 interpretable atomic evolution operations and supports multi-round compositions, enabling controlled generation of diverse, challenging, and realistic test samples. We conduct extensive experiments addressing four research questions on a broad set of open- and closed-source LLMs. Our results show that atomic operations cause an average accuracy drop of 7.283\\%, with structure-disrupting or misleading semantic edits causing the largest declines. Model sensitivities vary significantly for the same perturbation, and combining multiple evolution steps amplifies adversarial effects by up to 52.932\\%. These findings suggest current benchmarks may overestimate true model generalization and emphasize the need for evolution-aware robustness evaluation. Code and resources are available at: https://github.com/SYSUSELab/AutoEvoEval."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23735","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:53:11Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:53:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:53:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23731","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23731","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23731","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michel","familyName":"Meintz","name":"Meintz, Michel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Dubiński","name":"Dubiński, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Franziska","familyName":"Boenisch","name":"Boenisch, Franziska","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Dziedzic","name":"Dziedzic, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Radioactive Watermarks in Diffusion and Autoregressive Image Generative Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T11:08:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:13:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Image generative models have become increasingly popular, but training them requires large datasets that are costly to collect and curate. To circumvent these costs, some parties may exploit existing models by using the generated images as training data for their own models. In general, watermarking is a valuable tool for detecting unauthorized use of generated images. However, when these images are used to train a new model, watermarking can only enable detection if the watermark persists through training and remains identifiable in the outputs of the newly trained model - a property known as radioactivity. We analyze the radioactivity of watermarks in images generated by diffusion models (DMs) and image autoregressive models (IARs). We find that existing watermarking methods for DMs fail to retain radioactivity, as watermarks are either erased during encoding into the latent space or lost in the noising-denoising process (during the training in the latent space). Meanwhile, despite IARs having recently surpassed DMs in image generation quality and efficiency, no radioactive watermarking methods have been proposed for them. To overcome this limitation, we propose the first watermarking method tailored for IARs and with radioactivity in mind - drawing inspiration from techniques in large language models (LLMs), which share IARs' autoregressive paradigm. Our extensive experimental evaluation highlights our method's effectiveness in preserving radioactivity within IARs, enabling robust provenance tracking, and preventing unauthorized use of their generated images."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23731","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:53:06Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:53:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:53:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23725","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23725","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23725","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atharva","familyName":"Gundawar","name":"Gundawar, Atharva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Som","familyName":"Sagar","name":"Sagar, Som","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ransalu","familyName":"Senanayake","name":"Senanayake, Ransalu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PAC Bench: Do Foundation Models Understand Prerequisites for Executing Manipulation Policies?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T10:58:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:13:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) are increasingly pivotal for generalist robot manipulation, enabling tasks such as physical reasoning, policy generation, and failure detection. However, their proficiency in these high-level applications often assumes a deep understanding of low-level physical prerequisites, a capability that remains largely unverified. For robots to perform actions reliably, they must comprehend intrinsic object properties (e.g., material, weight), action affordances (e.g., graspable, stackable), and physical constraints (e.g., stability, reachability, or an object's state, such as being closed). Despite the widespread use of VLMs in manipulation tasks, we argue that off-the-shelf models may lack this granular, physically grounded understanding, as such prerequisites are often overlooked during training.\n To address this critical gap, we introduce PAC Bench, a comprehensive benchmark designed to systematically evaluate VLMs on their understanding of core Properties, Affordances, and Constraints (PAC) from a task executability perspective. PAC Bench features a diverse dataset with over 30,000 annotations, comprising 673 real-world images (115 object classes, 15 property types, and 1 to 3 affordances defined per class), 100 real-world humanoid-view scenarios, and 120 unique simulated constraint scenarios across four tasks.\n Our evaluations reveal significant gaps in the ability of current VLMs to grasp fundamental physical concepts, highlighting limitations in their suitability for reliable robot manipulation and pointing to key areas for targeted research. PAC Bench also serves as a standardized benchmark for rigorously evaluating physical reasoning in VLMs and guiding the development of more robust, physically grounded models for robotic applications.\n Project Page: https://pacbench.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23725","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:52:57Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:52:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:52:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23678","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23678","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23678","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rock Yuren","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Rock Yuren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K. J. Kevin","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, K. J. Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangbin","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Shangbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijia","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Weijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulia","familyName":"Tsvetkov","name":"Tsvetkov, Yulia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrey","familyName":"Heer","name":"Heer, Jeffrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katharina","familyName":"Reinecke","name":"Reinecke, Katharina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Interactive Reasoning: Visualizing and Controlling Chain-of-Thought Reasoning in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T10:00:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:10:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The output quality of large language models (LLMs) can be improved via \"reasoning\": generating segments of chain-of-thought (CoT) content to further condition the model prior to producing user-facing output. While these chains contain valuable information, they are verbose and lack explicit organization, making them tedious to review. Moreover, they lack opportunities for user feedback, such as to remove unwanted considerations, add desired ones, or clarify unclear assumptions. We introduce Interactive Reasoning, an interaction design that visualizes chain-of-thought outputs as a hierarchy of topics and enables user review and modification. We implement interactive reasoning in Hippo, a prototype for AI-assisted decision making in the face of uncertain trade-offs. In a user study with 16 participants, we find that interactive reasoning in Hippo allows users to quickly identify and interrupt erroneous generations, efficiently steer the model towards customized responses, and better understand both model reasoning and model outputs. Our work contributes to a new paradigm that incorporates user oversight into LLM reasoning processes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23678","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:51:50Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:51:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:51:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23667","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23667","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23667","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Junjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyi","familyName":"Xi","name":"Xi, Jingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuoyang","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zhuoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyu","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Junyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhua","familyName":"Ke","name":"Ke, Yuhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukun","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yukun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiaxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songxin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Songxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zejian","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Zejian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"L0: Reinforcement Learning to Become General Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T09:44:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:09:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Training large language models (LLMs) to act as autonomous agents for multi-turn, long-horizon tasks remains significant challenges in scalability and training efficiency. To address this, we introduce L-Zero (L0), a scalable, end-to-end training pipeline for general-purpose agents. Featuring a low-cost, extensible, and sandboxed concurrent agent worker pool, L0 lowers the barrier for applying reinforcement learning in complex environments. We also introduce NB-Agent, the agent scaffold within L0, which operates in a \"code-as-action\" fashion via a Read-Eval-Print-Loop (REPL). We evaluate L0 on factuality question-answering benchmarks. Our experiments demonstrate that a base model can develop robust problem-solving skills using solely Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR). On the Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct model, our method boosts accuracy on SimpleQA from 30 % to 80 % and on HotpotQA from 22 % to 41 %. We have open-sourced the entire L0 system, including our L0 series models, the NB-Agent, a complete training pipeline, and the corresponding training recipes on (https://github.com/cmriat/l0)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23667","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:51:34Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:51:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:51:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23663","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23663","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23663","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mario","familyName":"Koddenbrock","name":"Koddenbrock, Mario","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rudolf","familyName":"Hoffmann","name":"Hoffmann, Rudolf","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Brodmann","name":"Brodmann, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erik","familyName":"Rodner","name":"Rodner, Erik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Domain Robustness of Contrastive Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T09:39:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:09:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In real-world vision-language applications, practitioners increasingly rely on large, pretrained foundation models rather than custom-built solutions, despite limited transparency regarding their training data and processes. While these models achieve impressive performance on general benchmarks, their effectiveness can decline notably under specialized domain shifts, such as unique imaging conditions or environmental variations. In this work, we introduce Deepbench, a framework designed to assess domain-specific robustness of vision-language models (VLMs). Deepbench leverages a large language model (LLM) to generate realistic, context-aware image corruptions tailored to specific deployment domains without requiring labeled data. We evaluate a range of contrastive vision-language architectures and architectural variants across six real-world domains and observe substantial variability in robustness, highlighting the need for targeted, domain-aware evaluation. Deepbench is released as open-source software to support further research into domain-aware robustness assessment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Deepbench is available at https://github.com/ml-lab-htw/deepbench"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23663","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:51:28Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:51:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:51:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23639","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23639","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23639","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanpeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wanpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yicheng","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yijiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihao","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Zihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sipeng","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Sipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongqing","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Zongqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unified Multimodal Understanding via Byte-Pair Visual Encoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T09:08:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:08:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have made significant progress in vision-language understanding, yet effectively aligning different modalities remains a fundamental challenge. We present a framework that unifies multimodal understanding by applying byte-pair encoding to visual tokens. Unlike conventional approaches that rely on modality-specific encoders, our method directly incorporates structural information into visual tokens, mirroring successful tokenization strategies in text-only language models. We introduce a priority-guided encoding scheme that considers both frequency and spatial consistency, coupled with a multi-stage training procedure based on curriculum-driven data composition. These enhancements enable the transformer model to better capture cross-modal relationships and reason with visual information. Comprehensive experiments demonstrate improved performance across diverse vision-language tasks. By bridging the gap between visual and textual representations, our approach contributes to the advancement of more capable and efficient multimodal foundation models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23639","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:50:54Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:50:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:50:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23635","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23635","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23635","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mu-Chi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Mu-Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Po-Hsuan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Po-Hsuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangrui","familyName":"Ke","name":"Ke, Xiangrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chia-Heng","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Chia-Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chun Jason","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Chun Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shih-Hao","familyName":"Hung","name":"Hung, Shih-Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Building Private LLMs: Exploring Multi-Node Expert Parallelism on Apple Silicon for Mixture-of-Experts Large Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.6.4; I.2.7; I.2.11","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T09:04:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:08:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3649601.3698722","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have revolutionized Artificial Intelligence (AI) with significant advancements such as OpenAI's ChatGPT, Meta's Llama, and Databricks' DBRX. This paper addresses the cost and scalability challenges encountered when constructing private LLM systems for personal or small group services, as aimed by Apple Intelligence. A Mac Studio cluster with Apple's M2 Ultra chips is established as a cost-efficient solution to host and accelerate the pretrained DBRX model with the Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architecture. Our performance analysis reveal that parallel execution of the model's experts across two to four machine nodes significantly reduces inference time. We find that computation time for the experts is comparable to the communication time for exchanging their outputs, emphasizing the importance of network latency over bandwidth. We also observe significant management overhead due to Apple software stack's memory management logic. Based on these findings, we develop optimization schemes to eliminate the memory management overhead. As a result, the Mac Studio cluster is 1.15 times more cost-efficient than the state-of-the-art AI supercomputer with NVIDIA H100 GPUs. In addition, we construct a performance model to estimate system performance under varying configurations, and the model provides valuable insights for designing private LLM systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"International Conference on Research in Adaptive and Convergent Systems (RACS '24), November 5--8, 2024, Pompei, Italy"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23635","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-07-01T02:50:48Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:50:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:50:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23626","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23626","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23626","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"António","familyName":"Afonso","name":"Afonso, António","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iolanda","familyName":"Leite","name":"Leite, Iolanda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Sestini","name":"Sestini, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florian","familyName":"Fuchs","name":"Fuchs, Florian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konrad","familyName":"Tollmar","name":"Tollmar, Konrad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linus","familyName":"Gisslén","name":"Gisslén, Linus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-correcting Reward Shaping via Language Models for Reinforcement Learning Agents in Games"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T08:45:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:07:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning (RL) in games has gained significant momentum in recent years, enabling the creation of different agent behaviors that can transform a player's gaming experience. However, deploying RL agents in production environments presents two key challenges: (1) designing an effective reward function typically requires an RL expert, and (2) when a game's content or mechanics are modified, previously tuned reward weights may no longer be optimal. Towards the latter challenge, we propose an automated approach for iteratively fine-tuning an RL agent's reward function weights, based on a user-defined language based behavioral goal. A Language Model (LM) proposes updated weights at each iteration based on this target behavior and a summary of performance statistics from prior training rounds. This closed-loop process allows the LM to self-correct and refine its output over time, producing increasingly aligned behavior without the need for manual reward engineering. We evaluate our approach in a racing task and show that it consistently improves agent performance across iterations. The LM-guided agents show a significant increase in performance from $9\\%$ to $74\\%$ success rate in just one iteration. We compare our LM-guided tuning against a human expert's manual weight design in the racing task: by the final iteration, the LM-tuned agent achieved an $80\\%$ success rate, and completed laps in an average of $855$ time steps, a competitive performance against the expert-tuned agent's peak $94\\%$ success, and $850$ time steps."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages in total, 10 pages of main paper, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23626","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:50:35Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:50:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:50:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23618","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23618","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23618","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongdao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhongdao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guodongfang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Guodongfang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingjing","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Jingjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bailan","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Bailan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shifeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TurboVSR: Fantastic Video Upscalers and Where to Find Them"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T08:24:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:06:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion-based generative models have demonstrated exceptional promise in the video super-resolution (VSR) task, achieving a substantial advancement in detail generation relative to prior methods. However, these approaches face significant computational efficiency challenges. For instance, current techniques may require tens of minutes to super-resolve a mere 2-second, 1080p video. In this paper, we present TurboVSR, an ultra-efficient diffusion-based video super-resolution model. Our core design comprises three key aspects: (1) We employ an autoencoder with a high compression ratio of 32$\\times$32$\\times$8 to reduce the number of tokens. (2) Highly compressed latents pose substantial challenges for training. We introduce factorized conditioning to mitigate the learning complexity: we first learn to super-resolve the initial frame; subsequently, we condition the super-resolution of the remaining frames on the high-resolution initial frame and the low-resolution subsequent frames. (3) We convert the pre-trained diffusion model to a shortcut model to enable fewer sampling steps, further accelerating inference. As a result, TurboVSR performs on par with state-of-the-art VSR methods, while being 100+ times faster, taking only 7 seconds to process a 2-second long 1080p video. TurboVSR also supports image resolution by considering image as a one-frame video. Our efficient design makes SR beyond 1080p possible, results on 4K (3648$\\times$2048) image SR show surprising fine details."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICCV, 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23618","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:50:22Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:50:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:50:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23590","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23590","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23590","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zekai","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Zekai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaocheng","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Xiaocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihong","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Weihong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Libo","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Libo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruihan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ruihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baohang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Baohang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kui","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Kui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaowei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CAI: Caption-Sensitive Attention Intervention for Mitigating Object Hallucination in Large Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T07:52:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:05:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) have demonstrated powerful capabilities in interpreting visual information, they frequently produce content that deviates from visual information, leading to object hallucination. To tackle this, recent works mostly depend on expensive manual annotations and training cost, or significantly increase inference time. In this work, we observe that LVLMs' attention to visual information is significantly stronger when answering caption queries compared to non-caption queries. Inspired by this phenomenon, we propose Caption-sensitive Attention Intervention (CAI), a training-free, plug-and-play hallucination mitigation method that leverages the attention activation pattern in response to caption queries to enhance LVLMs' visual perception capability. Extensive experimental results across four benchmarks covering both discriminative and generative tasks, demonstrate that CAI achieves state-of-the-art (SOTA) hallucination mitigating performance only with minimal additional inference cost."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23590","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:49:42Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:49:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:49:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23589","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23589","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23589","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neta","familyName":"Shaul","name":"Shaul, Neta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Uriel","familyName":"Singer","name":"Singer, Uriel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Itai","familyName":"Gat","name":"Gat, Itai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaron","familyName":"Lipman","name":"Lipman, Yaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T07:51:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:04:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion and flow matching models have significantly advanced media generation, yet their design space is well-explored, somewhat limiting further improvements. Concurrently, autoregressive (AR) models, particularly those generating continuous tokens, have emerged as a promising direction for unifying text and media generation. This paper introduces Transition Matching (TM), a novel discrete-time, continuous-state generative paradigm that unifies and advances both diffusion/flow models and continuous AR generation. TM decomposes complex generation tasks into simpler Markov transitions, allowing for expressive non-deterministic probability transition kernels and arbitrary non-continuous supervision processes, thereby unlocking new flexible design avenues. We explore these choices through three TM variants: (i) Difference Transition Matching (DTM), which generalizes flow matching to discrete-time by directly learning transition probabilities, yielding state-of-the-art image quality and text adherence as well as improved sampling efficiency. (ii) Autoregressive Transition Matching (ARTM) and (iii) Full History Transition Matching (FHTM) are partially and fully causal models, respectively, that generalize continuous AR methods. They achieve continuous causal AR generation quality comparable to non-causal approaches and potentially enable seamless integration with existing AR text generation techniques. Notably, FHTM is the first fully causal model to match or surpass the performance of flow-based methods on text-to-image task in continuous domains. We demonstrate these contributions through a rigorous large-scale comparison of TM variants and relevant baselines, maintaining a fixed architecture, training data, and hyperparameters."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23589","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:49:41Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:49:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:49:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23576","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23576","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23576","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maria Carolina Cornelia","familyName":"Wit","name":"Wit, Maria Carolina Cornelia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Multi-Agent Defences Against Jailbreaking Attacks on Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T07:29:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:04:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have raised concerns about jailbreaking attacks, i.e., prompts that bypass safety mechanisms. This paper investigates the use of multi-agent LLM systems as a defence against such attacks. We evaluate three jailbreaking strategies, including the original AutoDefense attack and two from Deepleaps: BetterDan and JB. Reproducing the AutoDefense framework, we compare single-agent setups with two- and three-agent configurations. Our results show that multi-agent systems enhance resistance to jailbreaks, especially by reducing false negatives. However, its effectiveness varies by attack type, and it introduces trade-offs such as increased false positives and computational overhead. These findings point to the limitations of current automated defences and suggest directions for improving alignment robustness in future LLM systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23576","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:49:23Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:49:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:49:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23563","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23563","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23563","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huanjin","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Huanjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxing","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jiaxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yawen","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Yawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael K.","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Michael K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenzheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wenzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjie","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Wenjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xikun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xikun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dacheng","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Dacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MMReason: An Open-Ended Multi-Modal Multi-Step Reasoning Benchmark for MLLMs Toward AGI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T07:14:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:03:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning plays a crucial role in advancing Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) toward Artificial General Intelligence. However, existing MLLM benchmarks often fall short in precisely and comprehensively evaluating long-chain reasoning abilities from three key aspects: (1) lack of difficulty and diversity, (2) susceptibility to guessability and memorization, (3) inadequate assessment of intermediate reasoning steps. To fill this gap, we introduce MMReason, a new benchmark designed to precisely and comprehensively evaluate MLLM long-chain reasoning capability with diverse, open-ended, challenging questions. First, we curate challenging questions requiring multi-step reasoning from various fields (i.e., 6 disciplines) and multiple difficulty levels (i.e., from pre-university to university, and from foundational to competition tiers). Second, these questions are reformulated into an open-ended format and filtered using a multi-model voting technique to eliminate shortcut cases related to guessing and memorization, ensuring robust reasoning evaluations. Third, we annotate the questions with detailed step-by-step solutions, and design a reference-based ternary scoring mechanism to reliably assess intermediate reasoning steps. With MMReason, we benchmark popular leading MLLMs and provide an in-depth analysis of their reasoning capabilities. We hope MMReason will serve as a valuable resource for advancing MLLM reasoning research. Code will be available at https://github.com/HJYao00/MMReason."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Technical report"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23563","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:49:04Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:49:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:49:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23513","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23513","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23513","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixun","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Zixun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng-Jun","familyName":"Zha","name":"Zha, Zheng-Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ViewPoint: Panoramic Video Generation with Pretrained Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T04:33:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:00:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Panoramic video generation aims to synthesize 360-degree immersive videos, holding significant importance in the fields of VR, world models, and spatial intelligence. Existing works fail to synthesize high-quality panoramic videos due to the inherent modality gap between panoramic data and perspective data, which constitutes the majority of the training data for modern diffusion models. In this paper, we propose a novel framework utilizing pretrained perspective video models for generating panoramic videos. Specifically, we design a novel panorama representation named ViewPoint map, which possesses global spatial continuity and fine-grained visual details simultaneously. With our proposed Pano-Perspective attention mechanism, the model benefits from pretrained perspective priors and captures the panoramic spatial correlations of the ViewPoint map effectively. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method can synthesize highly dynamic and spatially consistent panoramic videos, achieving state-of-the-art performance and surpassing previous methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"https://becauseimbatman0.github.io/ViewPoint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23513","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:47:50Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:47:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:47:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23485","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23485","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23485","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haocheng","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Haocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaxiong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yaxiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yawen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyang","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Yuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junping","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Junping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enhong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Enhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Thought-Augmented Planning for LLM-Powered Interactive Recommender Agent"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-30T03:15:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:58:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Interactive recommendation is a typical information-seeking task that allows users to interactively express their needs through natural language and obtain personalized recommendations. Large language model-powered (LLM-powered) agents have become a new paradigm in interactive recommendations, effectively capturing users' real-time needs and enhancing personalized experiences. However, due to limited planning and generalization capabilities, existing formulations of LLM-powered interactive recommender agents struggle to effectively address diverse and complex user intents, such as intuitive, unrefined, or occasionally ambiguous requests. To tackle this challenge, we propose a novel thought-augmented interactive recommender agent system (TAIRA) that addresses complex user intents through distilled thought patterns. Specifically, TAIRA is designed as an LLM-powered multi-agent system featuring a manager agent that orchestrates recommendation tasks by decomposing user needs and planning subtasks, with its planning capacity strengthened through Thought Pattern Distillation (TPD), a thought-augmentation method that extracts high-level thoughts from the agent's and human experts' experiences. Moreover, we designed a set of user simulation schemes to generate personalized queries of different difficulties and evaluate the recommendations based on specific datasets. Through comprehensive experiments conducted across multiple datasets, TAIRA exhibits significantly enhanced performance compared to existing methods. Notably, TAIRA shows a greater advantage on more challenging tasks while generalizing effectively on novel tasks, further validating its superiority in managing complex user intents within interactive recommendation systems. The code is publicly available at:https://github.com/Alcein/TAIRA."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23485","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:47:10Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:47:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:47:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23329","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23329","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23329","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Parker","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Parker","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chenxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengxin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhengxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yipeng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wuyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiqin","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhiqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhenyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunlong","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yunlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sirui","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Sirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brandon Y.","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Brandon Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"IR3D-Bench: Evaluating Vision-Language Model Scene Understanding as Agentic Inverse Rendering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-29T17:02:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:49:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language models (VLMs) excel at descriptive tasks, but whether they truly understand scenes from visual observations remains uncertain. We introduce IR3D-Bench, a benchmark challenging VLMs to demonstrate understanding through active creation rather than passive recognition. Grounded in the analysis-by-synthesis paradigm, IR3D-Bench tasks Vision-Language Agents (VLAs) with actively using programming and rendering tools to recreate the underlying 3D structure of an input image, achieving agentic inverse rendering through tool use. This \"understanding-by-creating\" approach probes the tool-using generative capacity of VLAs, moving beyond the descriptive or conversational capacity measured by traditional scene understanding benchmarks. We provide a comprehensive suite of metrics to evaluate geometric accuracy, spatial relations, appearance attributes, and overall plausibility. Initial experiments on agentic inverse rendering powered by various state-of-the-art VLMs highlight current limitations, particularly in visual precision rather than basic tool usage. IR3D-Bench, including data and evaluation protocols, is released to facilitate systematic study and development of tool-using VLAs towards genuine scene understanding by creating."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project Page: https://ir3d-bench.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23329","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:43:26Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:43:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:43:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23225","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23225","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23225","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukito","familyName":"Tajima","name":"Tajima, Yukito","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nakamasa","familyName":"Inoue","name":"Inoue, Nakamasa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusuke","familyName":"Sekikawa","name":"Sekikawa, Yusuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ikuro","familyName":"Sato","name":"Sato, Ikuro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rio","familyName":"Yokota","name":"Yokota, Rio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Masked Gated Linear Unit"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-29T13:16:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:44:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Gated Linear Units (GLUs) have become essential components in the feed-forward networks of state-of-the-art Large Language Models (LLMs). However, they require twice as many memory reads compared to feed-forward layers without gating, due to the use of separate weight matrices for the gate and value streams. To address this bottleneck, we introduce Masked Gated Linear Units (MGLUs), a novel family of GLUs with an efficient kernel implementation. The core contribution of MGLUs include: (1) the Mixture of Element-wise Gating (MoEG) architecture that learns multiple binary masks, each determining gate or value assignments at the element level on a single shared weight matrix resulting in reduced memory transfer, and (2) FlashMGLU, a hardware-friendly kernel that yields up to a 19.7 $\\times$ inference-time speed-up over a naive PyTorch MGLU and is 47% more memory-efficient and 34% faster than standard GLUs despite added architectural complexity on an RTX5090 GPU. In LLM experiments, the Swish-activated variant SwiMGLU preserves its memory advantages while matching - or even surpassing - the downstream accuracy of the SwiGLU baseline."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23225","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:40:58Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:40:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:40:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23135","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23135","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23135","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinzhou","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yinzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RoboScape: Physics-informed Embodied World Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-29T08:19:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:39:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"World models have become indispensable tools for embodied intelligence, serving as powerful simulators capable of generating realistic robotic videos while addressing critical data scarcity challenges. However, current embodied world models exhibit limited physical awareness, particularly in modeling 3D geometry and motion dynamics, resulting in unrealistic video generation for contact-rich robotic scenarios. In this paper, we present RoboScape, a unified physics-informed world model that jointly learns RGB video generation and physics knowledge within an integrated framework. We introduce two key physics-informed joint training tasks: temporal depth prediction that enhances 3D geometric consistency in video rendering, and keypoint dynamics learning that implicitly encodes physical properties (e.g., object shape and material characteristics) while improving complex motion modeling. Extensive experiments demonstrate that RoboScape generates videos with superior visual fidelity and physical plausibility across diverse robotic scenarios. We further validate its practical utility through downstream applications including robotic policy training with generated data and policy evaluation. Our work provides new insights for building efficient physics-informed world models to advance embodied intelligence research. The code is available at: https://github.com/tsinghua-fib-lab/RoboScape."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23135","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:38:49Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:38:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:38:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23074","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23074","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23074","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyang","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Boyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fanye","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Fanye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueqi","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Yueqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingyao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Bingyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenzhao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Wenzhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiwen","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Counterfactually Decoupled Attention for Open-World Model Attribution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-29T03:25:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:36:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we propose a Counterfactually Decoupled Attention Learning (CDAL) method for open-world model attribution. Existing methods rely on handcrafted design of region partitioning or feature space, which could be confounded by the spurious statistical correlations and struggle with novel attacks in open-world scenarios. To address this, CDAL explicitly models the causal relationships between the attentional visual traces and source model attribution, and counterfactually decouples the discriminative model-specific artifacts from confounding source biases for comparison. In this way, the resulting causal effect provides a quantification on the quality of learned attention maps, thus encouraging the network to capture essential generation patterns that generalize to unseen source models by maximizing the effect. Extensive experiments on existing open-world model attribution benchmarks show that with minimal computational overhead, our method consistently improves state-of-the-art models by large margins, particularly for unseen novel attacks. Source code: https://github.com/yzheng97/CDAL."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICCV 2025. Code: \\url{https://github.com/yzheng97/CDAL}"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23074","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:37:22Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:37:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:37:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23025","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.23025","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.23025","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tejas","familyName":"Vaidhya","name":"Vaidhya, Tejas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayush","familyName":"Kaushal","name":"Kaushal, Ayush","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vineet","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Vineet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francis Couture","familyName":"Harpin","name":"Harpin, Francis Couture","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prashant","familyName":"Shishodia","name":"Shishodia, Prashant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Majid","familyName":"Behbahani","name":"Behbahani, Majid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuriy","familyName":"Nevmyvaka","name":"Nevmyvaka, Yuriy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irina","familyName":"Rish","name":"Rish, Irina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spectra 1.1: Scaling Laws and Efficient Inference for Ternary Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-28T22:13:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:33:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used across research and industry applications, yet their inference efficiency remains a significant challenge. As the computational power of modern GPU architectures continuously improves, their memory bandwidth and capacity have not scaled proportionally, creating a critical bottleneck during inference. To address this, we investigate ternary language models (TriLMs) that employ quantization-aware training to significantly reduce memory requirements. We first analyze the scalability of TriLMs by conducting a scaling law analysis, revealing that TriLMs benefit more from increasing training data than from scaling model parameters. Based on this observation, we introduce Spectra-1.1, an open suite of TriLMs trained on up to 1.2 trillion tokens, demonstrating sustained performance gains at scale. Furthermore, to improve inference efficiency, we propose novel 2-bit and 1.6-bit packing schemes for ternary weights, which demonstrate accelerated inference across various CPU architectures. Also, building on the 2-bit packing, we develop a GPU kernel called TriRun that accelerates end-to-end model inference by up to 5 times compared to floating-point baselines. To encourage further exploration and development of TriLMs, we will release the Spectra-1.1 suite and TriRun inference kernels. Overall, our work lays the foundation for building and deploying efficient LLMs, providing a valuable resource for the research community."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.23025","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:36:11Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:36:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:36:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22950","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22950","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22950","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Liangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huanyi","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Huanyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinhai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinhai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianjin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Tianjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengdi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Mengdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Infinite Sampling: Efficient and Stable Grouped RL Training for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-28T16:52:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:29:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Group-based reinforcement learning algorithms such as Group Reward Policy Optimization (GRPO) have proven effective for fine-tuning large language models (LLMs) with human feedback. However, generating and storing multiple responses per prompt incurs substantial memory overhead, especially as the sample group size increases, limiting scalability under constrained hardware.\n We propose Infinite Sampling, a framework that enables efficient and stable GRPO training by decoupling group size from GPU memory usage. It consists of: (1) micro sampling groups that decompose large groups into memory-feasible rounds; (2) continuous sampling that interleaves generation across groups to improve utilization; and (3) a length-aware scheduler combining token-conditioned sequence length prediction with a two-stage plan: global grouping via FPTAS and runtime refill via SJF.\n Experiments show that our Micro Sampling Groups reduce peak memory usage by over 50% compared to full-group decoding (e.g., from 21.55 GB to 10.64 GB on Qwen3-1.7B). Building on this, Infinite Sampling improves throughput by over 25% compared to the naive micro sampling group method, reducing decoding steps while maintaining full-length completions and memory usage. Our hybrid scheduling ensures efficient and stable GRPO training with larger groups under realistic GPU memory constraints."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22950","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:34:25Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:34:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:34:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22852","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22852","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22852","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yucheng","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yucheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junlan","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Junlan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijian","familyName":"Ou","name":"Ou, Zhijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Knowledge Augmented Finetuning Matters in both RAG and Agent Based Dialog Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-28T11:26:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T00:23:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have recently been applied to dialog systems. Despite making progress, LLMs are prone to errors in knowledge-intensive scenarios. Recently, approaches based on retrieval augmented generation (RAG) and agent have emerged to improve the factual accuracy by enhancing the LLMs with knowledge retrieved from external knowledge bases (KBs). This is mostly implemented by prompting the LLMs with instructions, examples and the retrieved knowledge. However, LLMs may have difficulty using the retrieved knowledge effectively for response generation, because they are not well trained to do such generation for specific domains. To mitigate this problem, we propose to finetune the LLMs in the RAG-based and agent-based systems with domain-specific data, together with domain-specific external knowledge, which is called knowledge augmented finetuning (KAFT). We base our study on the MobileCS2 dataset, a real-life customer service dialog dataset that features intensive knowledge interactions, to systematically compare the prompting and KAFT techniques in the RAG-based and agent-based systems. Experiment results show that KAFT substantially surpasses prompting in both RAG and agent systems, particularly in terms of factual accuracy. To the best of our knowledge, this paper represents the first solid empirical work to investigate the KAFT idea."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22852","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-01T02:32:06Z","registered":"2025-07-01T02:32:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-01T02:32:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22056","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22056","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22056","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Ziyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Leng","name":"Leng, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenbang","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Zhenbang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zora Zhiruo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zora Zhiruo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanyi","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Yanyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dehan","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Dehan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Universal Retrieval for Multimodal Trajectory Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T09:50:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:29:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Trajectory data, capturing human actions and environmental states across various modalities, holds significant potential for enhancing AI agent capabilities, particularly in GUI environments. However, how to model the representation of trajectory-level data presents a significant challenge that has not been systematically addressed amid explosive trajectory data growth. In this work, we introduce Multimodal Trajectory Retrieval, bridging the gap between universal retrieval and agent-centric trajectory modeling. We construct the Unified Agent Trajectory Dataset (UATD) from annotated demonstrations and states across diverse real-world scenarios. Based on this, we present GAE-Bench, a benchmark containing a large number of trajectory-based retrieval pairs. In addition, we propose GAE-Retriever, a multimodal retrieval framework that adopts vision-language models and incorporates optimized contrastive learning through a token selection and the GradCache mechanism. Comprehensive evaluations across multiple datasets show that GAE-Retriever consistently outperforms strong baselines in retrieval recall, highlighting its effectiveness in advancing multimodal trajectory retrieval."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 3 figures, accepted by Workshop on Computer-use Agents @ ICML 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22056","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:33:17Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:33:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:33:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22033","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22033","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22033","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongchao","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yongchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Bohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SiPipe: Bridging the CPU-GPU Utilization Gap for Efficient Pipeline-Parallel LLM Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T09:27:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:28:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As inference workloads for large language models (LLMs) scale to meet growing user demand, pipeline parallelism (PP) has become a widely adopted strategy for multi-GPU deployment, particularly in cross-node setups, to improve key-value (KV) cache capacity and inference throughput. However, PP suffers from inherent inefficiencies caused by three types of execution bubbles-load-imbalance, intra-stage, and inter-stage-which limit pipeline saturation. We present SiPipe, a heterogeneous pipeline design that improves throughput by leveraging underutilized CPU resources to offload auxiliary computation and communication. SiPipe incorporates three key techniques-CPU sampling, a token-safe execution model, and structure-aware transmission-to mitigate pipeline bubbles and improve execution efficiency. Across diverse LLMs, SiPipe achieves up to 2.1 times higher throughput, 43% lower per-token latency, and up to 23% higher average GPU utilization compared to the state-of-the-art vLLM under the same PP configuration, demonstrating its generality across LLMs and deployment scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22033","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:32:45Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:32:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:32:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21976","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21976","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21976","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuhan","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Shuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Lambert","name":"Lambert, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Jeon","name":"Jeon, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sakshum","familyName":"Kulshrestha","name":"Kulshrestha, Sakshum","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijing","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Yijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dragomir","familyName":"Anguelov","name":"Anguelov, Dragomir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxing","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Mingxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chiyu Max","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Chiyu Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SceneDiffuser++: City-Scale Traffic Simulation via a Generative World Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T07:35:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:23:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The goal of traffic simulation is to augment a potentially limited amount of manually-driven miles that is available for testing and validation, with a much larger amount of simulated synthetic miles. The culmination of this vision would be a generative simulated city, where given a map of the city and an autonomous vehicle (AV) software stack, the simulator can seamlessly simulate the trip from point A to point B by populating the city around the AV and controlling all aspects of the scene, from animating the dynamic agents (e.g., vehicles, pedestrians) to controlling the traffic light states. We refer to this vision as CitySim, which requires an agglomeration of simulation technologies: scene generation to populate the initial scene, agent behavior modeling to animate the scene, occlusion reasoning, dynamic scene generation to seamlessly spawn and remove agents, and environment simulation for factors such as traffic lights. While some key technologies have been separately studied in various works, others such as dynamic scene generation and environment simulation have received less attention in the research community. We propose SceneDiffuser++, the first end-to-end generative world model trained on a single loss function capable of point A-to-B simulation on a city scale integrating all the requirements above. We demonstrate the city-scale traffic simulation capability of SceneDiffuser++ and study its superior realism under long simulation conditions. We evaluate the simulation quality on an augmented version of the Waymo Open Motion Dataset (WOMD) with larger map regions to support trip-level simulation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to CVPR 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21976","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:31:24Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:31:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:31:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":70,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=%28doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21976%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22033%22+OR+